WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.978 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.978 --> 00:00:02.445 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.445 --> 00:00:04.401 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.401 --> 00:00:08.313 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:08.313 --> 00:00:11.260 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.360 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK, now
do you have any questions

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.140 align:middle line:84%
now that there's been
a little more delay?

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:15.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.460 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.660 align:middle line:90%
[SPEAKING WAMPANOAG]

00:00:19.660 --> 00:02:15.850 align:middle line:90%


00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.040 align:middle line:84%
So one of the things I
was telling you just now

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.590 align:middle line:84%
was that the language
that I was speaking

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.170 align:middle line:84%
had spent a long period
of time, many years,

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.140 align:middle line:84%
in fact, more than a century,
not being spoken at all.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.850 align:middle line:84%
So the Wampanoag are
engaged in trying to get

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.050 align:middle line:90%
their language spoken again.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:34.502 align:middle line:84%
But they're fighting
an uphill battle

00:02:34.502 --> 00:02:36.710 align:middle line:84%
because they don't have
native speakers to work with.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.793 align:middle line:84%
They're having to work
their way through documents

00:02:38.793 --> 00:02:42.820 align:middle line:84%
to try to figure out how
their language is spoken.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:46.270 align:middle line:84%
In being an Indigenous
language of the US

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:50.800 align:middle line:84%
that has spent some time
not being spoken at all,

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.900 align:middle line:90%
Wampanoag is not at all unusual.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.840 align:middle line:84%
So all of the Indigenous
languages of America

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.190 align:middle line:90%
are in some level of danger.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:05.750 align:middle line:84%
UNESCO has a series of
classifications for languages.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.770 align:middle line:84%
There are sort of different
levels of endangerment

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.560 align:middle line:84%
for languages, ranging from
languages that are vulnerable,

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.070 align:middle line:84%
that is, languages in which
there is some other dominant

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.070 align:middle line:84%
language around, but there
are still children learning

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.060 align:middle line:84%
to speak the language-- that's
the least level of concern--

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.310 align:middle line:84%
all the way down to critically
endangered languages.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.110 align:middle line:90%
Those are the languages--

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.780 align:middle line:84%
(I don't care that this Mac
cannot connect to iCloud.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.930 align:middle line:84%
Tell me later!) Those are the
languages in which the youngest

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:33.010 align:middle line:90%
speakers are elderly.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.997 align:middle line:84%
And they don't speak
it all that often.

00:03:34.997 --> 00:03:36.580 align:middle line:84%
And there are these
ranges in between,

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.040 align:middle line:84%
having to do with
how basically, how

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.520 align:middle line:84%
secure the chain of transmission
to the next generation

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.460 align:middle line:90%
for the language is.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.770 align:middle line:84%
All of the Indigenous
languages of this country

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:49.910 align:middle line:90%
are somewhere on this list.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.513 align:middle line:84%
So they range from the languages
that are merely vulnerable, all

00:03:53.513 --> 00:03:55.930 align:middle line:84%
the way down to the critically
endangered languages, which

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:57.230 align:middle line:90%
is most of them.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.250 align:middle line:84%
So most of the Indigenous
languages of this country

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.500 align:middle line:90%
are in some level of trouble.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.680 align:middle line:84%
Here's a map showing
the counties in which--

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:11.110 align:middle line:84%
this is census data
from 2006 to 2010--

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.030 align:middle line:84%
showing the counties
in which there

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:16.540 align:middle line:84%
are numbers of
speakers of Indigenous

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.760 align:middle line:90%
languages in different places.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:21.250 align:middle line:84%
You can see there are a
few places where there

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:22.810 align:middle line:90%
are large numbers of speakers.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.840 align:middle line:84%
By far, the largest native
language in this country

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.420 align:middle line:84%
is Navajo, which depending
on how you count,

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.780 align:middle line:84%
has somewhere around
120,000 speakers.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:35.080 align:middle line:84%
The other success
stories all have

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.080 align:middle line:84%
numbers of speakers ranging
in the 10 to 20,000.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.046 align:middle line:84%
So you can see some of the
examples on the map there.

00:04:42.046 --> 00:04:45.640 align:middle line:84%
You can also see that
some of these languages

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.740 align:middle line:84%
are spoken in
discontinuous places.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.010 align:middle line:84%
So Cherokee, for example,
has two communities

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.990 align:middle line:84%
where it's spoken,
one in Oklahoma

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:55.120 align:middle line:90%
and another further in the East.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.860 align:middle line:84%
If you know anything about
American history, you know why.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:59.890 align:middle line:84%
So this has to do with
the Trail of Tears,

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:03.760 align:middle line:84%
in which the Cherokee
in the early 1830s

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.610 align:middle line:84%
were compelled to
walk about 5,000 miles

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:10.000 align:middle line:84%
from their traditional
homelands, which

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.400 align:middle line:84%
were in Tennessee
and northern Alabama

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.300 align:middle line:84%
and northern Georgia, that
area all the way to Oklahoma.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.610 align:middle line:90%
Many of them died along the way.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.212 align:middle line:84%
So not all of them
went, I guess,

00:05:21.212 --> 00:05:23.170 align:middle line:84%
which is why there are
Cherokee speakers in two

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:23.837 align:middle line:90%
different areas.

00:05:23.837 --> 00:05:26.610 align:middle line:90%


00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.150 align:middle line:84%
So I just said, of
those languages,

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:31.760 align:middle line:90%
the largest is Navajo.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.620 align:middle line:84%
But although counting
speakers is the standard thing

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.860 align:middle line:84%
you do to figure out the
health, some kind of gauge

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.095 align:middle line:84%
of the health of
a language, it's

00:05:42.095 --> 00:05:45.000 align:middle line:84%
sort of a tricky thing to
do for several reasons.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.347 align:middle line:84%
One is that it's difficult to
find out how many people speak

00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:49.055 align:middle line:90%
a given language.

00:05:49.055 --> 00:05:53.340 align:middle line:90%


00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.440 align:middle line:84%
Particularly for languages
that are Indigenous or that are

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.670 align:middle line:84%
endangered, people have a
powerful motivation to want

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:05.417 align:middle line:84%
to say that they can speak the
language, even if they can't.

00:06:05.417 --> 00:06:07.250 align:middle line:84%
For a lot of these
languages, there are also

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.700 align:middle line:90%
forces in the other direction.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:13.870 align:middle line:84%
So a history of people being
punished traditionally,

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:16.610 align:middle line:84%
being taken to residential
schools and that kind of thing,

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.190 align:middle line:84%
in attempts to stop them from
speaking their Indigenous

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:20.768 align:middle line:84%
languages have
led people to want

00:06:20.768 --> 00:06:22.310 align:middle line:84%
to lie in the other
direction and say

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:23.930 align:middle line:84%
that they don't speak a
language, when in fact, they

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:24.430 align:middle line:90%
do.

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.930 align:middle line:84%
This-- all of this
makes it hard to count.

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:30.950 align:middle line:84%
But however you count, Navajo
is definitely the largest.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.860 align:middle line:84%
But that doesn't
make Navajo safe.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:38.540 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, 120,000,
is the upper range.

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:40.790 align:middle line:84%
If you're guessing how many
Navajo speakers there are,

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:43.297 align:middle line:90%
it's somewhere in 170,000.

00:06:43.297 --> 00:06:45.380 align:middle line:84%
That means that there are
roughly as many speakers

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:46.820 align:middle line:84%
of Navajo in this
country as there

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:52.040 align:middle line:90%
are of Malayalam or Albanian.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.500 align:middle line:84%
So it's by far the largest
Indigenous language.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.990 align:middle line:90%
But it's not large.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.890 align:middle line:84%
So it's not English
large or Spanish large.

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:07.130 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the larger
languages, but not huge.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:10.232 align:middle line:84%
And within the
Navajo reservation,

00:07:10.232 --> 00:07:11.690 align:middle line:84%
the Navajos have
been keeping track

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:15.920 align:middle line:84%
of how many Navajo
speakers enter,

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.900 align:middle line:84%
how many Navajos
enter kindergarten

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.850 align:middle line:90%
able to speak Navajo.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:23.120 align:middle line:84%
And those percentages
have been dropping ever

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:25.190 align:middle line:84%
since they started
keeping records.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:28.670 align:middle line:84%
They're now somewhere
less than half,

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:30.770 align:middle line:84%
which is bad news
for the future.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:33.186 align:middle line:84%
Even if the language
is currently large,

00:07:33.186 --> 00:07:35.061 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't mean it will
continue to be large.

00:07:35.061 --> 00:07:38.060 align:middle line:90%


00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:40.460 align:middle line:84%
I've been talking about
the United States,

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.920 align:middle line:84%
but the situation
in the United States

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.200 align:middle line:90%
is not particularly unusual.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:48.530 align:middle line:84%
So actually, if you
just count languages,

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:51.110 align:middle line:84%
if you look at the languages
of the world, most of them

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:52.070 align:middle line:90%
are endangered.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.530 align:middle line:84%
So not the ones that
you've heard of-- not

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:57.830 align:middle line:90%
English or Spanish or Mandarin.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:01.400 align:middle line:84%
But if you count
languages, there

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:04.520 align:middle line:84%
are something like 6,000 or
7,000 languages in the world,

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:08.480 align:middle line:84%
and the general expectation is
that we will lose at least half

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:12.170 align:middle line:84%
of them, and more pessimistic
people think more like 90%

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:18.180 align:middle line:84%
of them, once this
century is over with.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.770 align:middle line:84%
So here's a place
to pause and ask--

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:24.920 align:middle line:90%
we are in the middle of a shift.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.380 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of languages
in the world right now,

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:29.970 align:middle line:84%
and there are going
to be far fewer soon.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:32.929 align:middle line:84%
So the world is
changing from a world

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:37.220 align:middle line:84%
with a comparatively large
number of comparatively

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:40.940 align:middle line:84%
small languages to a world with
a comparatively small number

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:42.620 align:middle line:84%
of comparatively
large languages.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.530 align:middle line:84%
I hope I got the small and
larges right as I said that.

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:46.220 align:middle line:90%
I think I did.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:46.890 align:middle line:90%
Bless you.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:48.435 align:middle line:90%
That's what's happening.

00:08:48.435 --> 00:08:50.435 align:middle line:84%
How many of you think
that this is a good thing?

00:08:50.435 --> 00:08:55.730 align:middle line:90%


00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:59.600 align:middle line:84%
How many of you think
that this is a bad thing?

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.185 align:middle line:84%
How many of you think that
the question is too simple?

00:09:03.185 --> 00:09:05.560 align:middle line:84%
So those of you who think that
the question is too simple

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:08.770 align:middle line:84%
or those of you who think
that it's a bad thing, this

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:13.120 align:middle line:84%
is the part where I would
like to hear from you.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.000 align:middle line:84%
Are there-- and I always do
this at the beginning of class.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.820 align:middle line:84%
Where the heck-- someone is
hiding the chalk from me.

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:21.280 align:middle line:84%
Not me, specifically,
presumably, but from anybody

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.628 align:middle line:90%
who wants to teach here.

00:09:24.628 --> 00:09:26.170 align:middle line:84%
Wow, they're doing
a better job of it

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:28.000 align:middle line:90%
today than they usually do.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.560 align:middle line:84%
So, usually, if
I'm persistent, I

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:35.440 align:middle line:84%
can find some chalk,
but not today, no.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.060 align:middle line:90%
Wow.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:40.107 align:middle line:90%
So no chalk for me today.

00:09:40.107 --> 00:09:42.565 align:middle line:84%
I will just have to do today's
class by interpretive dance.

00:09:42.565 --> 00:09:45.070 align:middle line:90%


00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:46.390 align:middle line:90%
Wow.

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:49.510 align:middle line:84%
OK, no, I haven't hidden it
under my stuff or anything

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:51.555 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:09:51.555 --> 00:09:52.180 align:middle line:90%
That's strange.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:54.510 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let's just talk, then.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:57.010 align:middle line:84%
Those of you who think that
this question is too simple, who

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:58.927 align:middle line:84%
thinks that there might
be good things about--

00:09:58.927 --> 00:10:00.340 align:middle line:90%
oh my TAs are gesturing.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:02.298 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, if you wouldn't
mind, that would be great

00:10:02.298 --> 00:10:03.370 align:middle line:90%
if you can find some.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:06.250 align:middle line:84%
But for now, so
Enrico is on the case.

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:08.440 align:middle line:84%
He'll bring me some
chalk, if he can find it.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:09.100 align:middle line:90%
What the heck?

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:12.020 align:middle line:90%


00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:14.480 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so those of you
who think that there

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:16.280 align:middle line:84%
might be good things
about language loss

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:17.780 align:middle line:84%
or those of you who
think that there

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:20.030 align:middle line:84%
might be bad things
about language loss,

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:20.990 align:middle line:90%
let's hear from you.

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:21.740 align:middle line:90%
What do you think?

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:23.390 align:middle line:90%
Why do you think what you think?

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.110 align:middle line:90%


00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:28.480 align:middle line:84%
Anybody wish to
offer an opinion?

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:29.830 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:31.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: A lot of
[INAUDIBLE] stories

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:33.590 align:middle line:90%
just get lost in translation.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:35.555 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So a lot of stuff--

00:10:35.555 --> 00:10:37.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, can you
just say that again?

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:39.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Stories can
get lost in translation.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.270 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Stories
can get lost in translation.

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:44.947 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I think that's true.

00:10:44.947 --> 00:10:46.780 align:middle line:84%
I have a favorite example
of that, actually.

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:50.157 align:middle line:84%
Have I told you my story about
the movie Sleepless in Seattle?

00:10:50.157 --> 00:10:51.740 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the
dangers of teaching

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:54.655 align:middle line:84%
similar classes is that you
tell the same jokes over

00:10:54.655 --> 00:10:55.280 align:middle line:90%
and over again.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.470 align:middle line:84%
You lose track of
what you've said.

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:00.957 align:middle line:90%
So when I was--

00:11:00.957 --> 00:11:03.290 align:middle line:84%
you're much more sophisticated
than when I was your age.

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:06.020 align:middle line:84%
When I was your age, my
thought about that was always,

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:07.720 align:middle line:90%
no, but stuff can be translated.

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:10.940 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I can't read Russian
literature in Russian,

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:12.230 align:middle line:90%
but I can read it in English.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:15.230 align:middle line:84%
And so if Russian were
to vanish from the world,

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:18.260 align:middle line:84%
then we would still
have the literature.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:21.290 align:middle line:84%
I think there genuinely is a
phenomenon of stuff getting

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:23.460 align:middle line:84%
lost in translation, and
I have a favorite example,

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:26.480 align:middle line:84%
which is from the movie
Sleepless in Seattle.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:29.000 align:middle line:84%
Has anybody here seen the
movie Sleepless in Seattle?

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.590 align:middle line:84%
You have, wow, someone
who loves the classics.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:35.780 align:middle line:84%
So it's a romantic comedy
from the previous century,

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:37.910 align:middle line:84%
and I'm about to
spoil the ending,

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:40.490 align:middle line:84%
so if you're thinking
about watching it,

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:41.870 align:middle line:90%
just know that you're about to--

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:43.377 align:middle line:84%
I'm about to spoil
the ending, but I

00:11:43.377 --> 00:11:44.960 align:middle line:84%
don't feel too bad
about it because it

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:46.530 align:middle line:90%
is a romantic comedy after all.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.140 align:middle line:84%
And so it's not like
it's ever unclear

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.480 align:middle line:90%
what the ending is going to be.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:52.130 align:middle line:84%
So the end of the
movie-- the movie

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:55.370 align:middle line:84%
is about Tom Hanks and
Meg Ryan's characters.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.900 align:middle line:84%
They are destined
for each other,

00:11:57.900 --> 00:11:59.610 align:middle line:84%
but they live on
opposite coasts,

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:02.360 align:middle line:84%
and so lots of
wacky hijinks have

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:05.300 align:middle line:84%
to ensue before they
can actually meet.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:07.920 align:middle line:84%
At the end of the movie, they're
meeting for the first time,

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:08.420 align:middle line:90%
really.

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.910 align:middle line:84%
They're having their
first real conversation,

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.100 align:middle line:84%
and they're at the top of
the Empire State Building,

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:17.220 align:middle line:84%
and Tom Hanks' son from his
previous marriage is there too.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:21.090 align:middle line:84%
And after they have this brief
conversation in which they've

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.090 align:middle line:84%
just met for the first
time, Tom, at some point,

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:26.380 align:middle line:90%
says, "Well, we better go."

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:27.887 align:middle line:84%
And you see Meg
Ryan interpret that

00:12:27.887 --> 00:12:30.220 align:middle line:84%
as meaning that he and his
son are about to leave and go

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:33.100 align:middle line:84%
somewhere, and it was
nice meeting her, goodbye.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:34.840 align:middle line:90%
And then he says, "Shall we?"

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:37.390 align:middle line:84%
And holds out his hand
and she takes his hand

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:38.557 align:middle line:90%
and they walk off together.

00:12:38.557 --> 00:12:39.640 align:middle line:90%
They go into the elevator.

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:41.537 align:middle line:84%
You can see Tom Hanks'
son grinning broadly

00:12:41.537 --> 00:12:43.870 align:middle line:84%
and the music comes up, and
that's the end of the movie.

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:47.110 align:middle line:84%
There now I've spoiled the end
of the movie for you, sorry.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:51.190 align:middle line:84%
So that moment, where
he says "We better go,"

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:53.680 align:middle line:84%
and she misinterprets
him as meaning "My son

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:56.460 align:middle line:84%
and I are leaving now
and you can stay here,"

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:58.960 align:middle line:84%
and then it turns out that what
he means is "All three of us

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:02.560 align:middle line:84%
had better go," that's a
moment that relies crucially

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:05.890 align:middle line:84%
on the fact that English
pronouns are vague,

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:10.300 align:middle line:84%
that the first person
plural pronoun in English

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:14.270 align:middle line:84%
just means I, the speaker,
and some other people.

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:16.780 align:middle line:84%
And it's vague about whether
you, the person I'm talking to,

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:19.635 align:middle line:90%
is included in the group or not.

00:13:19.635 --> 00:13:21.010 align:middle line:84%
And there are
languages out there

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:22.400 align:middle line:90%
in which that is not true.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:24.730 align:middle line:84%
So in Lardil, for example,
the language from Australia

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:27.310 align:middle line:84%
that I worked on,
there's a distinction

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:29.950 align:middle line:84%
between what's called
inclusive and exclusive we.

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:35.110 align:middle line:84%
So if Tom Hanks means to
invite Meg Ryan along later,

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:37.930 align:middle line:84%
he's going to use the
pronoun "lag-ri-mol."

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:40.335 align:middle line:84%
And if he means to
leave without her,

00:13:40.335 --> 00:13:41.710 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be
him and his son,

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:45.165 align:middle line:84%
he'll use a completely
different pronoun, "yeddi."

00:13:45.165 --> 00:13:47.290 align:middle line:84%
Lardil actually has eight
different words for "we."

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.360 align:middle line:84%
It's possible to be
extremely precise about who's

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:50.207 align:middle line:90%
in the group or not.

00:13:50.207 --> 00:13:51.790 align:middle line:84%
There's another word
for "we" he could

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:54.370 align:middle line:84%
use to suggest to her that
she and he should now leave

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.800 align:middle line:84%
and his son can stay here at
the top of the Empire State

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:58.420 align:middle line:84%
Building, which--
that would have

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.730 align:middle line:90%
been a different kind of movie.

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:06.280 align:middle line:84%
So if you imagine a different
kind of world in which Lardil

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:07.960 align:middle line:84%
had taken over the
world, everyone

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:11.620 align:middle line:84%
spoke Lardil and English was
this endangered language that

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.840 align:middle line:84%
was down to a few
speakers and we

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:15.940 align:middle line:84%
were looking through
its literature, which

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:17.890 align:middle line:84%
included this movie,
Sleepless in Seattle,

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.140 align:middle line:84%
and we were going to see
whether we could translate it

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:24.412 align:middle line:84%
into Lardil or not, well, this
scene can't be translated.

00:14:24.412 --> 00:14:25.870 align:middle line:84%
So you want an
example of something

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:29.650 align:middle line:84%
that gets lost in translation,
here's an example.

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:32.987 align:middle line:84%
This scene relies
crucially on an ambiguity.

00:14:32.987 --> 00:14:34.570 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that the
English word "we"

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:38.020 align:middle line:84%
is ambiguous in a way
that can't be replicated

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:40.210 align:middle line:84%
in Lardil because Lardil
has two different pronouns

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:41.200 align:middle line:90%
for those things.

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:42.350 align:middle line:90%
Does this make sense?

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:45.760 align:middle line:84%
And so if they're both
fluent speakers of Lardil,

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:47.953 align:middle line:84%
and if she's paying
attention, and--

00:14:47.953 --> 00:14:49.870 align:middle line:84%
there's just no way to
replicate this problem.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:52.510 align:middle line:90%


00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:55.090 align:middle line:84%
So this is my pet
example of something

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:57.760 align:middle line:90%
that is lost in translation.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:00.970 align:middle line:84%
What gets lost in
translation is all the stuff

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:03.820 align:middle line:84%
that skillful writers
and storytellers

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:08.770 align:middle line:84%
do with relationships
between words, basically.

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:12.790 align:middle line:84%
So cases of ambiguity or
similarly phonological

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:14.470 align:middle line:90%
relationships between words--

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:17.140 align:middle line:84%
so rhyme and alliteration
and everything else

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:21.340 align:middle line:84%
that people do to create
verbal art of various kinds--

00:15:21.340 --> 00:15:22.990 align:middle line:84%
none of that stuff
survives very well.

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:25.210 align:middle line:84%
You can use footnotes to
try to explain it to people,

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:28.870 align:middle line:84%
and that will work about as
well as footnotes generally do.

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:31.350 align:middle line:84%
So yes, if you're
skeptical, as I

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:33.850 align:middle line:84%
was when I was your age, about
the concept of anything being

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:36.640 align:middle line:84%
lost in translation,
then yes, things can get

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:37.630 align:middle line:90%
lost in translation.

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:38.890 align:middle line:90%
I agree.

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:40.880 align:middle line:84%
It's a very long
response to that point.

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:43.270 align:middle line:84%
Anybody else wish to
make points about this?

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:44.770 align:middle line:84%
Not about being
lost in translation,

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:48.085 align:middle line:90%
but about language loss?

00:15:48.085 --> 00:15:48.585 align:middle line:90%
Pro?

00:15:48.585 --> 00:15:49.085 align:middle line:90%
Con?

00:15:49.085 --> 00:15:50.570 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:52.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't know whether
this is a pro or a con,

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:55.490 align:middle line:90%
It's sort of the same thing.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:59.570 align:middle line:84%
Languages are often
used as reflections

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:02.960 align:middle line:84%
of what a community
sees and thinks

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:05.750 align:middle line:84%
about the world and the
culture that they come from

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:13.240 align:middle line:84%
and how they interact with
the space and it kind of ties

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:16.060 align:middle line:84%
into the translation
thing, where it's

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:19.450 align:middle line:84%
really difficult to
capture that relationship

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:22.210 align:middle line:84%
that a certain language or
culture has in the world

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:24.790 align:middle line:84%
when you're translating it
into a different language.

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:31.560 align:middle line:84%
And if the original
language disappears,

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:39.990 align:middle line:84%
then you sort of lose an entire
perspective on life and events.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:41.460 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Thank you so much.

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:47.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Which feels like
a really significant loss

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:51.900 align:middle line:84%
to our general
knowledge of the world.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:58.350 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I'm going
to call this "linguistically

00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:04.692 align:middle line:84%
encoded cultural attitudes"
or something like that.

00:17:04.692 --> 00:17:06.359 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how to
phrase what you just

00:17:06.359 --> 00:17:08.520 align:middle line:84%
said, which you said so
nicely that I don't want

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:11.460 align:middle line:84%
to try to put it in a
bullet point on the board,

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:12.819 align:middle line:90%
and yet I feel that I have to.

00:17:12.819 --> 00:17:18.480 align:middle line:84%
So we're talking now about some
of the things that are lost--

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:20.849 align:middle line:90%
bad things about language death.

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:23.400 align:middle line:90%


00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:27.960 align:middle line:84%
So we need a nice, neutral
term for the phenomenon

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.730 align:middle line:84%
that we're talking
about, so I'll

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:32.820 align:middle line:84%
call it language death, which
is a nice, neutral term,

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:33.600 align:middle line:90%
it seems to me.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.195 align:middle line:90%


00:17:36.195 --> 00:17:38.070 align:middle line:84%
Are there any good things
about mass language

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:40.470 align:middle line:84%
death that anybody
wishes to talk about?

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:41.305 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Raquel?

00:17:41.305 --> 00:17:42.930 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, this
isn't a good thing

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:44.010 align:middle line:90%
about language death.

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:48.390 align:middle line:84%
It's just like, there are some
situations where if you were,

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:50.670 align:middle line:84%
I think, speaking
[INAUDIBLE] language

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.350 align:middle line:84%
that not many people
know, then you

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:55.170 align:middle line:84%
get a lot of [INAUDIBLE]
for mobility and integration

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:59.140 align:middle line:84%
with a more, I don't
know, potentially

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:02.400 align:middle line:84%
profitable or helpful
thing for you.

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:05.250 align:middle line:84%
If you wanted to get a
job or go to a city where

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:08.660 align:middle line:84%
there's a lot more
options for better living

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:11.690 align:middle line:84%
situation and learning
that language,

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:15.490 align:middle line:84%
and it's like abandoning
your own for the time being

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:18.443 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] that makes
your life better.

00:18:18.443 --> 00:18:20.360 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No,
that's a very good point.

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:22.198 align:middle line:84%
And I should have
come up with something

00:18:22.198 --> 00:18:23.740 align:middle line:84%
to call it other
than language death.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:25.668 align:middle line:90%
That's absolutely right.

00:18:25.668 --> 00:18:28.090 align:middle line:90%
Oh, pretty.

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:31.660 align:middle line:84%
So you're talking about one of
the primary causes of language

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:34.960 align:middle line:84%
endangerment, and
you're absolutely right.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:38.200 align:middle line:84%
And in a sense, the fact that
there is so much language death

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.180 align:middle line:84%
going on is a good sign of
something that's happening,

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:43.600 align:middle line:90%
which is economic mobility.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:46.480 align:middle line:90%


00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:52.150 align:middle line:84%
That is, you're absolutely right
that it is the case that people

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:57.040 align:middle line:84%
end up losing their
traditional languages in part

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:59.410 align:middle line:84%
because their traditional
languages are associated

00:18:59.410 --> 00:19:01.840 align:middle line:84%
with an economically
depressed area,

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:05.380 align:middle line:84%
and there's another
language that's associated

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:07.010 align:middle line:90%
with economic opportunity.

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:10.240 align:middle line:84%
And so people end up switching
to the language that's

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.080 align:middle line:84%
associated with
economic opportunity.

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:14.472 align:middle line:90%
So am I parodying your point?

00:19:14.472 --> 00:19:16.180 align:middle line:84%
I think that's the
point you were making,

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:17.170 align:middle line:90%
which I think is a good one.

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:18.462 align:middle line:90%
That is something that happens.

00:19:18.462 --> 00:19:20.830 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:20.830 --> 00:19:28.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think the
issue that's [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:31.190 align:middle line:84%
whether or not it's good or
bad that a language dies,

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:35.830 align:middle line:84%
but the method of which this
is accomplished because I think

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:38.920 align:middle line:84%
it's not unfair to say
that those kind of function

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:40.300 align:middle line:90%
of natural selection.

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:42.820 align:middle line:84%
If there's no reason to
speak this language anymore,

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:45.370 align:middle line:84%
there would be no advantage
to know this language other

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:47.360 align:middle line:84%
than, oh, it's cool that
I know this language.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.380 align:middle line:84%
I can speak it with
basically no one.

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:53.090 align:middle line:84%
It's going to fall
out, but whether or not

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:55.940 align:middle line:84%
this is my choice
because the culture just

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:59.390 align:middle line:84%
decided to adapt and
assimilate with others

00:19:59.390 --> 00:20:03.260 align:middle line:84%
or whether this is by
force because they were put

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:05.690 align:middle line:84%
in a situation where their
language would have no choice

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:08.772 align:middle line:90%
to proliferate [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:08.772 --> 00:20:09.730 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Right.

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:11.210 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to put this
in the good column,

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:13.168 align:middle line:84%
because what you're doing
is talking about ways

00:20:13.168 --> 00:20:19.750 align:middle line:84%
in which perhaps we
shouldn't be so upset,

00:20:19.750 --> 00:20:21.820 align:middle line:84%
and you just raised a
good point to which I

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:24.910 align:middle line:90%
want to amplify a little bit.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:27.490 align:middle line:84%
There are cases in which
languages are endangered

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:32.020 align:middle line:84%
because there was another group
that deliberately attempted

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:33.220 align:middle line:90%
to destroy them.

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:35.350 align:middle line:84%
So that's-- I alluded
to that earlier.

00:20:35.350 --> 00:20:38.530 align:middle line:90%


00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:43.330 align:middle line:84%
So we've had this series of
bloodcurdling discoveries

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:45.160 align:middle line:90%
about residential schools--

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:49.330 align:middle line:84%
lots of graves of children at
these schools where Indigenous

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:54.490 align:middle line:84%
children were sent in order to
make them civilized and this

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:56.330 align:middle line:90%
is not ancient history.

00:20:56.330 --> 00:20:58.420 align:middle line:84%
So there are still
people alive today

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:01.600 align:middle line:84%
who are survivors
of these schools

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:04.690 align:middle line:84%
and tell these awful
stories about being

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:07.540 align:middle line:84%
punished for speaking
their native languages.

00:21:07.540 --> 00:21:13.840 align:middle line:84%
And those events have
consequences for generations

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:14.600 align:middle line:90%
to come.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:17.860 align:middle line:84%
So there are
elderly people today

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:20.140 align:middle line:84%
who I have heard
talking about what

00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:22.960 align:middle line:84%
it was like being a small
child in these places

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:24.940 align:middle line:84%
where they were
afraid for their lives

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:27.520 align:middle line:84%
if they spoke their native
languages, the languages

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:31.390 align:middle line:84%
that they went into the school,
in some cases monolingual in,

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.632 align:middle line:84%
and they spent the
rest of their lives

00:21:33.632 --> 00:21:35.590 align:middle line:84%
being frightened to speak
their native language

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:36.820 align:middle line:90%
and unwilling to speak it.

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:38.830 align:middle line:84%
They didn't pass it
on to their children,

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:41.200 align:middle line:84%
so those residential
schools were

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:43.450 align:middle line:84%
very effective in
their stated goal

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:46.030 align:middle line:84%
of eliminating the
culture of the people

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:48.700 align:middle line:90%
that they were processing.

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:52.870 align:middle line:84%
That's a disturbingly
common story.

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:54.150 align:middle line:90%
So it happened in Canada.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:55.290 align:middle line:90%
It happened in the US.

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:57.582 align:middle line:84%
It certainly happened
in Australia.

00:21:57.582 --> 00:21:59.040 align:middle line:84%
When I was working
with the Lardil,

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:02.580 align:middle line:84%
I was working with people
who remembered when there

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:05.610 align:middle line:84%
was a dormitory where children
were sent and taken away

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:08.400 align:middle line:84%
from their families
and kept there.

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:11.280 align:middle line:84%
One consequence of that
was that the people

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:15.430 align:middle line:84%
that I learned Lardil
from were all old men,

00:22:15.430 --> 00:22:21.420 align:middle line:84%
because when they had
the dormitory system,

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:25.638 align:middle line:84%
they were more lax with the
boys than with the girls.

00:22:25.638 --> 00:22:27.180 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps some of you
have heard people

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:30.180 align:middle line:84%
say this-- "girls are delicate
and need to be protected,"

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:32.380 align:middle line:84%
in this case from
their families.

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:35.280 align:middle line:84%
And so the girls were
kept under lock and key

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:37.068 align:middle line:90%
and never allowed to go home.

00:22:37.068 --> 00:22:38.860 align:middle line:84%
The boys were allowed
to go home sometimes.

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:41.010 align:middle line:90%
And so they learned some Lardil.

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:46.440 align:middle line:84%
The women of that generation
were guarded more closely

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.220 align:middle line:90%
than that.

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:51.000 align:middle line:84%
So that's one extreme,
but then there's

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:54.630 align:middle line:90%
this other extreme, which is--

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:57.300 align:middle line:90%


00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:59.850 align:middle line:84%
I guess you and Raquel
have both now alluded to--

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:04.770 align:middle line:84%
cases where you have
a language and it's

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:09.180 align:middle line:84%
not currently under the
kind of monstrous threat

00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:11.650 align:middle line:84%
that I've just
been talking about.

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:15.060 align:middle line:84%
But if you grow up
in a community where

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:17.400 align:middle line:84%
this language is
spoken, you can see

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:20.070 align:middle line:84%
that there's another language
nearby that will give you

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:21.850 align:middle line:90%
more economic opportunity.

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:24.870 align:middle line:90%
And so you go for that one.

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:29.610 align:middle line:84%
That's the other kind
of case to talk about.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:31.710 align:middle line:84%
There's some kind
of relationship

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:33.750 align:middle line:90%
between these cases, of course.

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:36.810 align:middle line:84%
So it's often the case
that the languages that

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:39.990 align:middle line:84%
were deliberately
discriminated against,

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:43.920 align:middle line:84%
attempts to deliberately
destroy them--

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:45.780 align:middle line:84%
those are, then, the
languages that tend

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:47.130 align:middle line:90%
to be economically depressed.

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:51.900 align:middle line:84%
There's some kind of connection
between these kinds of cases.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:54.210 align:middle line:90%
And I guess we could ask--

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:57.390 align:middle line:84%
so obviously, the choice to
speak a different language

00:23:57.390 --> 00:24:00.270 align:middle line:84%
than your native one because
there's economic opportunity

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:04.117 align:middle line:84%
somewhere else seems
more like a free choice

00:24:04.117 --> 00:24:06.450 align:middle line:84%
than the choice to speak
another language because you'll

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:09.900 align:middle line:84%
be beaten to death if you
speak your native language.

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:12.030 align:middle line:84%
That's definitely
more of a free choice.

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:14.190 align:middle line:84%
But it's not a
completely free choice.

00:24:14.190 --> 00:24:16.620 align:middle line:84%
You're being pushed in
a particular direction

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:19.110 align:middle line:84%
by economic forces,
it seems to me.

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:20.220 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Faith?

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This is a case of
my own family, not necessarily

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:25.680 align:middle line:84%
just different languages,
although that did happen

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:27.630 align:middle line:84%
with my grandparents'
generation because they

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:30.060 align:middle line:84%
were hit for speaking
Spanish in schools.

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:33.360 align:middle line:84%
But I don't know if
you'd call it a dialect

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:35.940 align:middle line:84%
or just like the way
that people speak.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:38.040 align:middle line:84%
I know my mom and just
my family in general,

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:39.130 align:middle line:90%
they speak a certain way.

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:41.190 align:middle line:84%
But when they're in a
more professional setting

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:42.850 align:middle line:84%
that's not with
family, they drop

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:45.905 align:middle line:84%
a lot of the characteristics of
their speech that are natural.

00:24:45.905 --> 00:24:47.655 align:middle line:84%
And I thought that was
always really weird

00:24:47.655 --> 00:24:48.716 align:middle line:84%
because it was like
they're speaking

00:24:48.716 --> 00:24:49.475 align:middle line:84%
a different language
when they're

00:24:49.475 --> 00:24:50.390 align:middle line:90%
in the home environment.

00:24:50.390 --> 00:24:52.040 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Great,
and this connects

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:53.510 align:middle line:84%
to some of the stuff we
were talking about earlier,

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:55.135 align:middle line:84%
about it being
difficult to distinguish

00:24:55.135 --> 00:24:57.240 align:middle line:84%
a language from a dialect,
and maybe not worth it,

00:24:57.240 --> 00:25:00.080 align:middle line:84%
that people have their
own ways of speaking.

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:01.580 align:middle line:84%
And you're absolutely
right, they

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:03.500 align:middle line:84%
feel the need to
suppress them sometimes

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:05.870 align:middle line:84%
under professional
circumstances.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:09.140 align:middle line:84%
This is indeed the way that
languages and dialects--

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:13.880 align:middle line:84%
whatever those are--
become extinct.

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:17.745 align:middle line:84%
I've heard-- there's a great
activist for the Passamaquoddy

00:25:17.745 --> 00:25:19.370 align:middle line:84%
language, a guy named
Wayne Newell, who

00:25:19.370 --> 00:25:23.030 align:middle line:84%
died a few years ago now,
spent his whole life working

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:26.570 align:middle line:84%
to try to keep Passamaquoddy
education going in the schools

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:28.760 align:middle line:84%
to the extent that
Passamaquoddy is still spoken.

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:31.210 align:middle line:90%
There's a lot to thank him for.

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:32.960 align:middle line:84%
I once heard him give
a speech in which he

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:36.680 align:middle line:84%
said that he could remember,
as a child, a time when--

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:41.480 align:middle line:84%
and so when he was a child, it
would have been in the '40s, I

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:43.910 align:middle line:90%
guess, or the '50s when--

00:25:43.910 --> 00:25:47.840 align:middle line:84%
so Passamaquoddy is spoken
in Northeastern Maine.

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:49.820 align:middle line:84%
So he can remember,
as a child, a time

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:52.850 align:middle line:84%
when racism directed
at the Passamaquoddy

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:56.960 align:middle line:84%
was so bad that there was
never any hope of Passamaquoddy

00:25:56.960 --> 00:26:00.320 align:middle line:84%
people getting a job in
the English-speaking world.

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:02.930 align:middle line:84%
So Passamaquoddy children
were used as farm laborers.

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:05.600 align:middle line:90%
So they picked potatoes.

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:08.150 align:middle line:84%
So there was agricultural
work for some kinds of people.

00:26:08.150 --> 00:26:10.175 align:middle line:84%
But getting an
office job in which

00:26:10.175 --> 00:26:12.050 align:middle line:84%
you needed to be able
to speak English-- that

00:26:12.050 --> 00:26:17.540 align:middle line:84%
was just not in the cards for
anybody from that generation.

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:20.600 align:middle line:84%
And he said, now
there is still racism

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:23.100 align:middle line:84%
directed at the Passamaquoddy,
but it's nowhere near as bad.

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:24.600 align:middle line:84%
And it is possible
for Passamaquoddy

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:27.350 align:middle line:84%
to become nurses and
teachers and work

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:30.830 align:middle line:84%
in grocery stores
and so on, which

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:32.280 align:middle line:90%
is obviously a good thing.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:37.070 align:middle line:84%
But the perverse fact is that
when the racism was worse,

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:39.380 align:middle line:84%
there was a kind of
protection for their language.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:42.980 align:middle line:84%
Nobody was impelled to think,
oh, if I speak English better,

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:43.850 align:middle line:90%
I'll get a good job.

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:45.350 align:middle line:84%
You weren't going
to get a good job.

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:48.710 align:middle line:84%
It wasn't in the cards for
you if you were Passamaquoddy.

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:51.680 align:middle line:90%
So that force was never in play.

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:55.010 align:middle line:84%
And he was saying, and now
it is, thanks to something

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:56.900 align:middle line:84%
which is clearly a
good thing, which

00:26:56.900 --> 00:26:58.940 align:middle line:84%
is that Passamaquoddy
people have

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:02.450 align:middle line:84%
the choice of a certain
kind of economic freedom

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:05.090 align:middle line:84%
that their elders
might not have had.

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:08.060 align:middle line:90%


00:27:08.060 --> 00:27:09.750 align:middle line:90%
You all are making good points.

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:11.430 align:middle line:84%
Anybody else wish to
make a good point?

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:12.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:17.370 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does that 50% to 90%
account for the possibility

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:20.140 align:middle line:84%
of bilingual
divergence and dialects

00:27:20.140 --> 00:27:23.420 align:middle line:90%
becoming [INAUDIBLE] spaces?

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:25.010 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
one of the reasons

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:28.280 align:middle line:84%
that I say approximately
6,000 languages,

00:27:28.280 --> 00:27:31.170 align:middle line:84%
and then I say 50% to 90%--
there are lots of reasons.

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:33.440 align:middle line:84%
One is that, as I said
before, counting languages is

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:35.180 align:middle line:90%
kind of a fool's game.

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:38.120 align:middle line:84%
It's unclear what
counts as a language.

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:42.170 align:middle line:84%
And another just has to do with
how optimistic or pessimistic

00:27:42.170 --> 00:27:43.610 align:middle line:90%
you are.

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:46.310 align:middle line:84%
I think the people who are
making these numbers are not

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:49.820 align:middle line:84%
thinking about the
generation of new languages--

00:27:49.820 --> 00:27:52.040 align:middle line:90%
so further dialectal divergence.

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:53.540 align:middle line:84%
And to be fair to
those people, I

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:57.110 align:middle line:84%
think that's happening
at a much slower rate

00:27:57.110 --> 00:28:01.035 align:middle line:84%
than the rate at which
languages are becoming extinct.

00:28:01.035 --> 00:28:02.660 align:middle line:84%
I guess another thing
to say about that

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:06.770 align:middle line:84%
is that dialectal divergence and
the creation of new languages--

00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:08.450 align:middle line:90%
it is indeed happening.

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:11.600 align:middle line:84%
There are even examples where
we have it on videotape.

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:14.840 align:middle line:84%
For reasons of the
calendar I ended up

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:17.510 align:middle line:84%
having to get rid of something,
get rid of some topic

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:20.768 align:middle line:84%
to talk about, and I decided
to get rid of signed languages,

00:28:20.768 --> 00:28:22.685 align:middle line:84%
so I was going to do a
day on signed languages

00:28:22.685 --> 00:28:24.575 align:middle line:90%
and I had to take that out.

00:28:24.575 --> 00:28:26.450 align:middle line:84%
One of the things I
would have told you about

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:28.760 align:middle line:84%
is the creation of
Nicaraguan sign language,

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:30.170 align:middle line:90%
which is indeed a new language.

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:34.130 align:middle line:84%
It was created in
Nicaragua, hence the name.

00:28:34.130 --> 00:28:36.590 align:middle line:84%
When the Sandinistas
took over-- so there

00:28:36.590 --> 00:28:38.900 align:middle line:84%
was a revolution in Nicaragua,
and for the first time,

00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:40.880 align:middle line:90%
there were schools for the deaf.

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:44.540 align:middle line:84%
So prior to that, deaf
people stayed at home

00:28:44.540 --> 00:28:47.390 align:middle line:84%
and did what's sometimes
called home signs.

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:50.420 align:middle line:84%
So they would develop sign
languages of their own,

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:53.330 align:middle line:84%
interacting with the hearing
people who were around them.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.640 align:middle line:84%
When schools for the
deaf were first created,

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:58.310 align:middle line:84%
you suddenly had all these
deaf people in one place.

00:28:58.310 --> 00:29:01.010 align:middle line:84%
And there's video
of this happening.

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:02.730 align:middle line:90%
They created a language.

00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:05.510 align:middle line:84%
So Nicaraguan sign
was a new language

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:11.170 align:middle line:84%
that was created in the 1980s,
and people saw it happen.

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:12.920 align:middle line:84%
So there are cases of
new languages coming

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:16.310 align:middle line:84%
into existence, but it's
happening a lot less quickly

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:17.750 align:middle line:90%
than language death.

00:29:17.750 --> 00:29:19.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's right.

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:21.440 align:middle line:90%


00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:23.440 align:middle line:84%
Are there points people
wish to make about this?

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:28.460 align:middle line:90%


00:29:28.460 --> 00:29:29.765 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:29:29.765 --> 00:29:32.440 align:middle line:90%


00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:35.530 align:middle line:84%
One other reason,
maybe, to care--

00:29:35.530 --> 00:29:41.230 align:middle line:84%
there is, in Indigenous
communities, very serious--

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:43.420 align:middle line:84%
so for a lot of the
economic reasons that we've

00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:46.810 align:middle line:84%
been talking about, but also
for some of the cultural reasons

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:50.200 align:middle line:84%
that we've been
talking about, there's

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:54.370 align:middle line:84%
a long standing problem
with high suicide

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:58.690 align:middle line:84%
rates among the youth in a
lot of Indigenous communities.

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:00.790 align:middle line:84%
There was one study--
this was from 2007.

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:02.950 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if it
has been followed up--

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:08.080 align:middle line:84%
in British Columbia up in
Canada, which established,

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:11.320 align:middle line:84%
I think to everybody's
satisfaction,

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:16.480 align:middle line:84%
that there was a correlation
between having a language that

00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:17.920 align:middle line:90%
was comparatively strong--

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:21.670 align:middle line:84%
so British Columbia has
many Indigenous languages,

00:30:21.670 --> 00:30:25.272 align:middle line:84%
all of which are in
some level of danger,

00:30:25.272 --> 00:30:27.730 align:middle line:84%
but the communities in which
the language was comparatively

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:30.070 align:middle line:84%
strong were the languages
in which the youth suicide

00:30:30.070 --> 00:30:31.540 align:middle line:90%
rate was lowest.

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:35.110 align:middle line:84%
It was the communities in which
the language was on its way

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:38.320 align:middle line:84%
out-- those were the languages
in which it was highest.

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:43.510 align:middle line:84%
You all are all scientifically
trained, so it's reasonable

00:30:43.510 --> 00:30:46.430 align:middle line:84%
now to wonder about the
direction of causation,

00:30:46.430 --> 00:30:49.390 align:middle line:84%
so whether it's the communities
in which the language is

00:30:49.390 --> 00:30:51.940 align:middle line:84%
strong are also the
communities in which life is,

00:30:51.940 --> 00:30:54.940 align:middle line:84%
in other ways,
better, and so those

00:30:54.940 --> 00:30:58.330 align:middle line:84%
are the communities in which
the youth suicide rate is low,

00:30:58.330 --> 00:31:01.060 align:middle line:84%
whereas if the language
is on its way out,

00:31:01.060 --> 00:31:03.880 align:middle line:84%
those are the languages
in which the young are

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:07.690 align:middle line:84%
more likely to feel that
their lives are hopeless.

00:31:07.690 --> 00:31:10.030 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, this is one kind of
reason to care about this.

00:31:10.030 --> 00:31:16.150 align:middle line:84%
There's arguably a
life-saving possibility

00:31:16.150 --> 00:31:19.690 align:middle line:84%
to having a language
that is your own-- that

00:31:19.690 --> 00:31:21.790 align:middle line:84%
is, being able to
look at the world

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:24.144 align:middle line:84%
and think of it as
a world in which,

00:31:24.144 --> 00:31:27.230 align:middle line:84%
OK, maybe you are growing up
in an Indigenous community.

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:31.750 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's not an
economically vibrant place,

00:31:31.750 --> 00:31:33.550 align:middle line:84%
but you have something
which is yours

00:31:33.550 --> 00:31:36.400 align:middle line:90%
and in which you are the expert.

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:38.390 align:middle line:84%
There's apparently
something about that,

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:41.720 align:middle line:84%
which is, as I said
potentially life-saving.

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:44.710 align:middle line:84%
So just another thing to think
about as we think about this.

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:49.542 align:middle line:90%


00:31:49.542 --> 00:31:51.250 align:middle line:84%
Thank you for talking
with me about this.

00:31:51.250 --> 00:31:53.130 align:middle line:84%
I always feel
weird asking people

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:55.750 align:middle line:84%
to speak frankly their opinions
about this kind of thing.

00:31:55.750 --> 00:31:57.787 align:middle line:84%
But in the years that
I've taught this class,

00:31:57.787 --> 00:31:59.370 align:middle line:84%
I felt less weird
because I discovered

00:31:59.370 --> 00:32:01.370 align:middle line:84%
that MIT undergrads are
willing to speak frankly

00:32:01.370 --> 00:32:03.270 align:middle line:84%
about their opinions,
even if it's clear

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:06.120 align:middle line:84%
what their professor's
opinion is,

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:08.610 align:middle line:84%
because the fact is that it's
a very complicated question.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:11.590 align:middle line:84%
There are all kinds of factors
interacting with each other,

00:32:11.590 --> 00:32:15.657 align:middle line:84%
and I think we've sketched some
of them in a way that's useful.

00:32:15.657 --> 00:32:17.490 align:middle line:84%
One thing that we're
doing in our department

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:19.440 align:middle line:84%
that I wanted to
tell you about--

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:22.390 align:middle line:84%
we have a master's
program in our department.

00:32:22.390 --> 00:32:27.360 align:middle line:84%
It's called the MITILI, the
Indigenous Language Initiative,

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:29.760 align:middle line:84%
which I like to
pronounce "mightily."

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:33.420 align:middle line:84%
It's a master's program
for people from communities

00:32:33.420 --> 00:32:36.180 align:middle line:84%
with endangered languages who
can come to our department

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:40.140 align:middle line:84%
to get master's degrees
in linguistics, the theory

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:42.570 align:middle line:84%
being that getting master's
degrees in linguistics,

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:45.120 align:middle line:84%
the process of getting
educated in that way,

00:32:45.120 --> 00:32:48.870 align:middle line:84%
will help them with the creation
of pedagogical materials,

00:32:48.870 --> 00:32:51.270 align:middle line:84%
if that's what they want
to do, or help them have

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:53.910 align:middle line:84%
the tools to analyze
their language the way

00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:55.620 align:middle line:90%
a linguist would.

00:32:55.620 --> 00:32:58.470 align:middle line:84%
So this program has been running
for a number of years now.

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:01.008 align:middle line:84%
We currently have a Navajo
student in the program

00:33:01.008 --> 00:33:02.550 align:middle line:84%
and a Mi'kmaq student
in the program,

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:04.980 align:middle line:84%
had a number of Wampanoag
students and Passamaquoddy.

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:06.732 align:middle line:90%
We had an Inupiaq student.

00:33:06.732 --> 00:33:08.190 align:middle line:84%
Next year, we have
a student coming

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:12.990 align:middle line:84%
who's from a minority group
in Bangladesh, the Marma, who

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:14.550 align:middle line:84%
speak a Tibeto-Burman
language, which

00:33:14.550 --> 00:33:16.440 align:middle line:90%
is severely underdescribed.

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:19.650 align:middle line:84%
So she's very concerned with
the future for the language

00:33:19.650 --> 00:33:22.943 align:middle line:84%
and wants to come and get
some tools to help work on it.

00:33:22.943 --> 00:33:24.360 align:middle line:84%
So this is one
kind of thing we're

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:29.110 align:middle line:84%
doing in our department
about this worldwide fact.

00:33:29.110 --> 00:33:32.520 align:middle line:84%
I guess one of the reasons
that I'd like to spend today

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:36.750 align:middle line:84%
talking about this in this
class is that when I tell people

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:39.540 align:middle line:84%
I have had this experience, that
I tell people that there are

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:43.800 align:middle line:84%
many endangered languages, and
the reaction I sometimes get

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:44.370 align:middle line:90%
is surprise.

00:33:44.370 --> 00:33:46.620 align:middle line:84%
People will say, oh, I've
heard of endangered species,

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:48.840 align:middle line:84%
but I didn't know there
were endangered languages.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:50.465 align:middle line:84%
So I didn't want any
of you to come out

00:33:50.465 --> 00:33:51.717 align:middle line:90%
of this class being like that.

00:33:51.717 --> 00:33:52.800 align:middle line:90%
So all of you should know.

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:54.450 align:middle line:84%
There are many
endangered languages--

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:56.070 align:middle line:90%
in fact, most of them.

00:33:56.070 --> 00:33:59.580 align:middle line:90%


00:33:59.580 --> 00:34:02.660 align:middle line:84%
Shifting gears, I
started this class

00:34:02.660 --> 00:34:04.660 align:middle line:84%
by talking to you for a
little bit in Wampanoag.

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:07.870 align:middle line:84%
I wanted to tell you a little
bit about how that work has

00:34:07.870 --> 00:34:11.050 align:middle line:84%
gone, like why I think I know
how to pronounce Wampanoag

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:14.110 align:middle line:84%
words, even though we have no
audiotape of native speakers

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:16.510 align:middle line:90%
pronouncing it.

00:34:16.510 --> 00:34:18.670 align:middle line:90%
The big sources for Wampanoag--

00:34:18.670 --> 00:34:21.790 align:middle line:84%
there is a Bible
translation into Wampanoag.

00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:23.800 align:middle line:84%
It is the first
Bible that was ever

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:25.600 align:middle line:90%
published in this hemisphere.

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:28.540 align:middle line:84%
It's a Wampanoag Bible that
was published in Cambridge.

00:34:28.540 --> 00:34:30.880 align:middle line:84%
First edition was
in 1663, and then we

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:36.100 align:middle line:84%
work with the second
edition, which is from 1685.

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:39.770 align:middle line:84%
There's a colleague of mine
here at MIT who tells me

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:42.429 align:middle line:84%
she has a friend
at the Smithsonian

00:34:42.429 --> 00:34:46.870 align:middle line:84%
who is sometimes called on
to supply bibles for swearing

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:48.370 align:middle line:90%
in ceremonies.

00:34:48.370 --> 00:34:50.420 align:middle line:84%
Like when the
president is sworn in,

00:34:50.420 --> 00:34:53.620 align:middle line:84%
they want the president to swear
the oath of office on a Bible.

00:34:53.620 --> 00:34:56.020 align:middle line:84%
And the Smithsonian
is sometimes contacted

00:34:56.020 --> 00:34:59.140 align:middle line:84%
to provide the Bible that
President Lincoln used

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:00.260 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:35:00.260 --> 00:35:02.710 align:middle line:84%
And that they are sometimes
asked for the first Bible

00:35:02.710 --> 00:35:04.575 align:middle line:90%
published in the US.

00:35:04.575 --> 00:35:05.950 align:middle line:84%
They always are
like, no, no, you

00:35:05.950 --> 00:35:11.110 align:middle line:84%
don't want that, believe
me, because it's this one.

00:35:11.110 --> 00:35:14.560 align:middle line:84%
It was translated
by John Eliot, who

00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:22.300 align:middle line:84%
was a missionary from England
who came here in the 1600s, who

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:26.260 align:middle line:84%
translated the Bible
as part of an effort

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:29.740 align:middle line:84%
to convert the Wampanoag to
Christianity, an effort that

00:35:29.740 --> 00:35:31.720 align:middle line:90%
was successful in a lot of ways.

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:36.430 align:middle line:84%
They created a lot of
Christian Wampanoag.

00:35:36.430 --> 00:35:40.580 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of documentation
of the whole process,

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:45.400 align:middle line:84%
including these lists
of the questions

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:49.090 align:middle line:84%
that they got asked as they were
trying to spread Christianity

00:35:49.090 --> 00:35:50.710 align:middle line:90%
to the Wampanoag.

00:35:50.710 --> 00:35:52.180 align:middle line:84%
One of the questions,
I remember,

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:56.410 align:middle line:84%
was whether Jesus Christ had
been baptized by full immersion

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:57.310 align:middle line:90%
or by sprinkling.

00:35:57.310 --> 00:35:59.710 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why they
wanted to know that.

00:35:59.710 --> 00:36:05.680 align:middle line:84%
Another was why, if baptism
cleanses people of sin,

00:36:05.680 --> 00:36:08.050 align:middle line:84%
when we baptize
our children, they

00:36:08.050 --> 00:36:11.200 align:middle line:84%
asked, why do our children
begin to sin again

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:13.030 align:middle line:90%
after they have been baptized--

00:36:13.030 --> 00:36:16.362 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes immediately
after they have been baptized?

00:36:16.362 --> 00:36:18.820 align:middle line:84%
We don't have any record of
the answers to these questions,

00:36:18.820 --> 00:36:21.580 align:middle line:84%
but the questions are
kind of interesting.

00:36:21.580 --> 00:36:24.130 align:middle line:84%
Another question that they
got asked over and over again

00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:26.320 align:middle line:84%
was whether God, if
the Wampanoag were

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:29.020 align:middle line:84%
to switch to worshiping God,
whether God would protect them

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:30.250 align:middle line:90%
from the plague.

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:36.100 align:middle line:84%
So the Wampanoag were suffering
from communicable diseases that

00:36:36.100 --> 00:36:38.260 align:middle line:84%
were brought over
by the colonists

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:42.970 align:middle line:90%
inadvertently, which wiped out--

00:36:42.970 --> 00:36:46.750 align:middle line:84%
it's another one of these large
range of possible percentages

00:36:46.750 --> 00:36:48.880 align:middle line:84%
of the Wampanoag, but
there were entire towns

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:50.710 align:middle line:90%
that were wiped out completely.

00:36:50.710 --> 00:36:53.890 align:middle line:84%
It's possible that the death
rate was as high as 80% or 90%.

00:36:53.890 --> 00:36:56.650 align:middle line:90%
It was just a catastrophe.

00:36:56.650 --> 00:36:58.510 align:middle line:90%
The Wampanoag weren't savages.

00:36:58.510 --> 00:37:00.700 align:middle line:84%
They had an
understanding of medicine

00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:02.560 align:middle line:84%
that was at least as
sophisticated as that

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:04.480 align:middle line:84%
of the pilgrims in
the 1600s, which

00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:06.220 align:middle line:84%
is to say not all
that sophisticated,

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:07.840 align:middle line:84%
but they knew some
stuff about how

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:10.540 align:middle line:84%
to treat people who
were sick, and all of it

00:37:10.540 --> 00:37:15.550 align:middle line:84%
was useless because they didn't
have any immunity to what they

00:37:15.550 --> 00:37:16.490 align:middle line:90%
were up against.

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:19.120 align:middle line:84%
So one very frequent question
in these lists of questions

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:21.370 align:middle line:90%
is, will God save our lives?

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:23.200 align:middle line:84%
Will he protect us
from the plague?

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:25.630 align:middle line:84%
And as I say, we don't have
any record of the answers,

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:30.048 align:middle line:84%
and I wish I knew what they
were told when they said that.

00:37:30.048 --> 00:37:32.090 align:middle line:84%
The Eliot Bible is a really
interesting document.

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:34.900 align:middle line:84%
I have been reading it very
slowly and carefully for years

00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:37.450 align:middle line:84%
now, trying to put the
language back together.

00:37:37.450 --> 00:37:42.910 align:middle line:84%
It's also a document in which
Eliot is clearly attempting

00:37:42.910 --> 00:37:46.300 align:middle line:84%
not only to spread
Christianity, but to suppress

00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:49.310 align:middle line:90%
the existing religious beliefs.

00:37:49.310 --> 00:37:53.050 align:middle line:84%
This is one verse that kind
of highlights that for me.

00:37:53.050 --> 00:37:57.280 align:middle line:84%
This is a verse from the
book of Exodus, so 22:18.

00:37:57.280 --> 00:38:00.160 align:middle line:84%
If you're familiar
with the Old Testament,

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:03.130 align:middle line:84%
it includes a lot
of confrontations

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:06.970 align:middle line:84%
between the priests
of the one true God

00:38:06.970 --> 00:38:09.400 align:middle line:84%
and then priests
of other religions

00:38:09.400 --> 00:38:11.740 align:middle line:84%
that were there that
were in the Middle East

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:15.910 align:middle line:84%
at the time, in which the
priests of the one true God

00:38:15.910 --> 00:38:18.878 align:middle line:84%
show that their god is more
powerful than the other gods

00:38:18.878 --> 00:38:19.420 align:middle line:90%
around there.

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:20.862 align:middle line:84%
This is kind of a
recurring theme.

00:38:20.862 --> 00:38:23.320 align:middle line:84%
The priests of the other gods
are sometimes called priests,

00:38:23.320 --> 00:38:25.278 align:middle line:84%
but they're often called
other kinds of things.

00:38:25.278 --> 00:38:29.200 align:middle line:84%
They're called like witches
or wizards or whatever.

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:32.980 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the verses that
exemplifies the attitude you're

00:38:32.980 --> 00:38:34.480 align:middle line:84%
supposed to have
toward these people

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:35.740 align:middle line:90%
and you should kill them.

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:37.375 align:middle line:84%
So "Thou shalt
not suffer a witch

00:38:37.375 --> 00:38:41.290 align:middle line:84%
to live," or, in Wampanoag,
that's [SPEAKING WAMPANOAG]..

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:45.880 align:middle line:84%
That last word, "pawaw,"
is actually the origin

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:47.650 align:middle line:90%
of the English word "powwow."

00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:51.580 align:middle line:84%
So it refers to a person
who was responsible

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:54.280 align:middle line:84%
for religious ceremony
in Wampanoag-- so

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:55.802 align:middle line:90%
traditional religious ceremony.

00:38:55.802 --> 00:38:57.760 align:middle line:84%
When that word was first
borrowed into English,

00:38:57.760 --> 00:38:59.270 align:middle line:84%
it meant what it
means in Wampanoag.

00:38:59.270 --> 00:39:05.040 align:middle line:84%
So the earliest English
citations of the word "powwow"

00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:06.005 align:middle line:90%
are all about--

00:39:06.005 --> 00:39:07.380 align:middle line:84%
the powwows were
getting together

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:08.580 align:middle line:90%
and doing their ceremonies.

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:09.997 align:middle line:84%
It refers to these
people who were

00:39:09.997 --> 00:39:12.690 align:middle line:84%
doing traditional ceremonies,
people who, today, are

00:39:12.690 --> 00:39:14.740 align:middle line:90%
sometimes called pipe carriers.

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:16.080 align:middle line:90%
So that's what this verse means.

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:19.590 align:middle line:84%
It means you should kill
the powwows, the people who

00:39:19.590 --> 00:39:21.900 align:middle line:90%
do your traditional religions.

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:26.630 align:middle line:84%
Not only should you ignore them,
but you should let them die.

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:29.300 align:middle line:84%
There's the Bible-- a bunch
of other religious texts.

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:31.340 align:middle line:90%
So here's a catechism.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:33.890 align:middle line:84%
There's a Wampanoag version
of the Lord's Prayer

00:39:33.890 --> 00:39:36.455 align:middle line:84%
and a bunch of questions
and answers, which

00:39:36.455 --> 00:39:39.080 align:middle line:84%
include things like "Why is God
called"-- here's the first one.

00:39:39.080 --> 00:39:40.400 align:middle line:90%
"Why is God called the father?"

00:39:40.400 --> 00:39:43.940 align:middle line:84%
Well, because he created
us and all people.

00:39:43.940 --> 00:39:45.410 align:middle line:90%
And other texts--

00:39:45.410 --> 00:39:49.562 align:middle line:84%
Eliot created a logic
textbook in Wampanoag.

00:39:49.562 --> 00:39:51.020 align:middle line:84%
Eliot, I mean,
whatever you thought

00:39:51.020 --> 00:39:57.230 align:middle line:84%
of his goal of eliminating
Wampanoag religious beliefs--

00:39:57.230 --> 00:40:01.712 align:middle line:84%
maybe it's clear by now that
I'm not actually a fan--

00:40:01.712 --> 00:40:03.170 align:middle line:84%
whatever you think
of that goal, he

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:05.170 align:middle line:84%
went about it in the most
sensible way possible.

00:40:05.170 --> 00:40:09.413 align:middle line:84%
So he preached in Wampanoag,
so he learned Wampanoag

00:40:09.413 --> 00:40:10.580 align:middle line:90%
well enough to preach in it.

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:13.100 align:middle line:84%
He did a lot of visiting
in Wampanoag communities,

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:15.230 align:middle line:84%
probably spreading
diseases as he did so,

00:40:15.230 --> 00:40:17.300 align:middle line:90%
but he didn't know that.

00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:21.860 align:middle line:84%
And he also ordained a
bunch of Wampanoag ministers

00:40:21.860 --> 00:40:25.055 align:middle line:84%
to go and spread the gospel
among the Wampanoag themselves.

00:40:25.055 --> 00:40:26.930 align:middle line:84%
So if he had been a
different kind of person,

00:40:26.930 --> 00:40:30.230 align:middle line:84%
he would have insisted that
only white people educated

00:40:30.230 --> 00:40:31.940 align:middle line:84%
in England could
spread the gospel,

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:33.470 align:middle line:84%
but he was not that
kind of person.

00:40:33.470 --> 00:40:35.870 align:middle line:84%
He was the kind of person
who wanted the locals

00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:40.010 align:middle line:84%
to know enough to do the
evangelism themselves.

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:42.860 align:middle line:84%
And that's what the logic
primer was part of--

00:40:42.860 --> 00:40:45.320 align:middle line:84%
so the idea was if
they learned logic,

00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:47.390 align:middle line:84%
that would help them
construct logical arguments

00:40:47.390 --> 00:40:51.290 align:middle line:84%
and convince people
to become Christians.

00:40:51.290 --> 00:40:55.310 align:middle line:84%
And I don't have an example
here of this, I don't think.

00:40:55.310 --> 00:40:57.080 align:middle line:84%
But it's an
introduction to logic,

00:40:57.080 --> 00:41:00.770 align:middle line:84%
as logic was understood in the
1600s, where the examples are

00:41:00.770 --> 00:41:05.390 align:middle line:84%
all syllogism that involve
proving things from the Bible.

00:41:05.390 --> 00:41:08.060 align:middle line:90%


00:41:08.060 --> 00:41:09.917 align:middle line:84%
It's a very
interesting document,

00:41:09.917 --> 00:41:11.750 align:middle line:84%
and the result is that
we know the Wampanoag

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:14.620 align:middle line:84%
word for syllogism, which
I wasn't expecting to.

00:41:14.620 --> 00:41:15.620 align:middle line:90%
It's on this page.

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:19.040 align:middle line:84%
It's [WAMPANOAG],, which
means a short speech,

00:41:19.040 --> 00:41:21.020 align:middle line:84%
something like that, I
guess because the point

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:23.990 align:middle line:84%
of a syllogism is that
it boils something

00:41:23.990 --> 00:41:27.620 align:middle line:84%
that would be a long
argument down to its basics.

00:41:27.620 --> 00:41:30.073 align:middle line:90%
That's why it's short.

00:41:30.073 --> 00:41:31.490 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
a bunch of what

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:32.660 align:middle line:90%
are called native writings.

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:34.550 align:middle line:84%
These are documents
that were written

00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:40.250 align:middle line:84%
by Native speakers of Wampanoag,
who learned to read and write.

00:41:40.250 --> 00:41:43.400 align:middle line:84%
The literacy rate at one point
was comparable to the literacy

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:45.860 align:middle line:90%
rate among the whites.

00:41:45.860 --> 00:41:49.160 align:middle line:84%
The native writings are
mostly legal documents

00:41:49.160 --> 00:41:50.460 align:middle line:90%
of various kinds.

00:41:50.460 --> 00:41:54.080 align:middle line:84%
So this is a bill
of sale for a house,

00:41:54.080 --> 00:41:57.920 align:middle line:84%
I guess where David Oks is
selling his house and all

00:41:57.920 --> 00:42:01.130 align:middle line:84%
that he has in this
place to Isaac Tuhkemen.

00:42:01.130 --> 00:42:04.700 align:middle line:84%
And here's the date and
the signature and so on.

00:42:04.700 --> 00:42:06.290 align:middle line:84%
I wish that one of
the native writings

00:42:06.290 --> 00:42:08.880 align:middle line:84%
were a novel or a diary
or something like that.

00:42:08.880 --> 00:42:10.130 align:middle line:90%
But there's nothing like that.

00:42:10.130 --> 00:42:12.167 align:middle line:90%
It's all stuff like this.

00:42:12.167 --> 00:42:14.000 align:middle line:84%
Some of it is kind of
heartbreaking to read.

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:16.790 align:middle line:84%
Here's a petition written
by the people in Mashpee

00:42:16.790 --> 00:42:21.260 align:middle line:84%
to the commissioners
in Boston, asking

00:42:21.260 --> 00:42:25.790 align:middle line:84%
them to intervene because
the white people were

00:42:25.790 --> 00:42:27.650 align:middle line:90%
taking their land.

00:42:27.650 --> 00:42:30.690 align:middle line:84%
And it has lines in it
like this one "Truly,

00:42:30.690 --> 00:42:31.730 align:middle line:90%
we think it is this--

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:33.710 align:middle line:84%
we, the poor Indians,
shall soon not

00:42:33.710 --> 00:42:36.440 align:middle line:84%
have any place to reside
because these Englishmen--"

00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:38.270 align:middle line:84%
by which they mean
the white people--

00:42:38.270 --> 00:42:40.338 align:middle line:90%
"are troubling us very much."

00:42:40.338 --> 00:42:42.630 align:middle line:84%
They were wrong about this,
actually, as it turned out.

00:42:42.630 --> 00:42:44.172 align:middle line:84%
There are still
Wampanoag in Mashpee.

00:42:44.172 --> 00:42:46.890 align:middle line:84%
There's one place
where they still live.

00:42:46.890 --> 00:42:50.350 align:middle line:84%
So we have all these sources,
but the sources, of course,

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:53.070 align:middle line:84%
are documents from
the 1600s, which

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:55.170 align:middle line:84%
was a time when it was
the mark of a gentleman,

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:57.900 align:middle line:84%
even in English, not to spell
a word the same way twice.

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:00.690 align:middle line:84%
So the spelling
is pretty erratic,

00:43:00.690 --> 00:43:02.700 align:middle line:84%
and so we have to do a
lot of detective work

00:43:02.700 --> 00:43:04.770 align:middle line:84%
to try to figure out
what exactly it was

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:06.990 align:middle line:90%
that they were writing down.

00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:10.770 align:middle line:84%
Some of the detective
work involves

00:43:10.770 --> 00:43:13.680 align:middle line:84%
discovering sound
correspondences of the kind

00:43:13.680 --> 00:43:15.647 align:middle line:84%
that we were talking
about the other day

00:43:15.647 --> 00:43:17.730 align:middle line:84%
when we were talking about
historical linguistics.

00:43:17.730 --> 00:43:19.620 align:middle line:84%
So we were saying
one of the things

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:23.640 align:middle line:84%
that you learn is that when
you have two related languages,

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:26.407 align:middle line:84%
it's possible to state these
law-like generalizations--

00:43:26.407 --> 00:43:27.990 align:middle line:84%
wherever this language
has this sound,

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:29.962 align:middle line:90%
this language has this sound.

00:43:29.962 --> 00:43:31.170 align:middle line:90%
We can do this for Wampanoag.

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:34.350 align:middle line:84%
Here's Wampanoag compared with
a closely-related Algonquian

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:35.790 align:middle line:90%
language called Delaware.

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:38.190 align:middle line:84%
You can see from
these data here,

00:43:38.190 --> 00:43:40.650 align:middle line:84%
in the middle there,
in the brackets,

00:43:40.650 --> 00:43:43.447 align:middle line:84%
I have the spellings from the
actual Wampanoag documents.

00:43:43.447 --> 00:43:45.780 align:middle line:84%
And then, over on the right,
we have the Wampanoag words

00:43:45.780 --> 00:43:48.810 align:middle line:84%
the way that we spell them
today, which you can see here

00:43:48.810 --> 00:43:51.180 align:middle line:84%
is that wherever
Delaware has a long "ah,"

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:54.630 align:middle line:84%
Wampanoag has a vowel that
sure looks like it's nasal.

00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:58.290 align:middle line:84%
So the vowel is getting
written with an "n" or an "m"

00:43:58.290 --> 00:43:58.890 align:middle line:90%
next to it.

00:43:58.890 --> 00:44:02.490 align:middle line:84%
So that word for "yesterday,
we think is "wa-nonk-ial."

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:05.700 align:middle line:84%
Or we have "non-pee,"
which is again, or "skonk,"

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:08.933 align:middle line:84%
which is skunk, or it's
the word that we borrowed,

00:44:08.933 --> 00:44:10.350 align:middle line:84%
one of the words
that we borrowed.

00:44:10.350 --> 00:44:12.060 align:middle line:84%
So here are a bunch
of examples where

00:44:12.060 --> 00:44:14.160 align:middle line:84%
wherever Delaware
has a long "ah,"

00:44:14.160 --> 00:44:18.690 align:middle line:84%
Wampanoag has what looks
like a nasal vowel, and "aw,"

00:44:18.690 --> 00:44:21.980 align:middle line:84%
which we spell like this,
an "o" with a hat on it.

00:44:21.980 --> 00:44:24.260 align:middle line:84%
Wherever Delaware has
a short "ah," well,

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:26.600 align:middle line:84%
it looks as though Wampanoag
has probably something

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:27.560 align:middle line:90%
like a short "ah."

00:44:27.560 --> 00:44:32.540 align:middle line:84%
So there's a vowel that gets
spelled with an "o" or an "a."

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:36.398 align:middle line:84%
And so our guess is that
this is also an "ah" sound.

00:44:36.398 --> 00:44:37.940 align:middle line:84%
I was just complaining
about the fact

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:39.448 align:middle line:90%
that the spelling is variable.

00:44:39.448 --> 00:44:41.990 align:middle line:84%
Of course, the best thing would
be if the Wampanoag documents

00:44:41.990 --> 00:44:43.580 align:middle line:90%
were all written in the IPA.

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:46.550 align:middle line:84%
Then I would know exactly
what they were spelling.

00:44:46.550 --> 00:44:49.080 align:middle line:84%
The fact that the spelling is
kind of variable is, in a way,

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:50.120 align:middle line:90%
the next best thing.

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:53.120 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if they always spelled
this vowel with the letter a,

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:55.700 align:middle line:84%
then I'd have to worry about
whether they were spelling

00:44:55.700 --> 00:44:59.630 align:middle line:84%
"ah," or "aaa," or "ay," any
variety of things that we

00:44:59.630 --> 00:45:00.860 align:middle line:90%
spelled with that vowel.

00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:02.930 align:middle line:84%
But the fact that they
spell this vowel sometimes

00:45:02.930 --> 00:45:05.262 align:middle line:84%
with an "a" and sometimes
with an "o" kind of

00:45:05.262 --> 00:45:06.470 align:middle line:90%
narrows it down a little bit.

00:45:06.470 --> 00:45:07.928 align:middle line:84%
I don't have to
worry so much about

00:45:07.928 --> 00:45:09.050 align:middle line:90%
whether it's "ay" or "aa."

00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:11.930 align:middle line:84%
It's probably something
more like "ah" or "au."

00:45:11.930 --> 00:45:14.060 align:middle line:84%
So where a Delaware
has a long "ah,"

00:45:14.060 --> 00:45:15.890 align:middle line:84%
Wampanoag has a
nasal vowel, an "on."

00:45:15.890 --> 00:45:17.390 align:middle line:84%
Wherever Delaware
has a short "ah,"

00:45:17.390 --> 00:45:20.840 align:middle line:90%
Wampanoag seems to have an "ah."

00:45:20.840 --> 00:45:22.832 align:middle line:84%
Knowing that is handy
when we're looking

00:45:22.832 --> 00:45:25.290 align:middle line:84%
at words where it's hard to
tell what kind of "ah" we have.

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:26.810 align:middle line:84%
So here's a very
common word that

00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:29.450 align:middle line:84%
means too
much/excessively, and these

00:45:29.450 --> 00:45:32.850 align:middle line:84%
are a bunch of Wampanoag
spellings from the documents.

00:45:32.850 --> 00:45:34.850 align:middle line:84%
And because the
next consonant is

00:45:34.850 --> 00:45:37.880 align:middle line:84%
an "m" it's hard to know,
just looking at that spelling,

00:45:37.880 --> 00:45:41.900 align:middle line:84%
am I looking at an "au" or
am I looking at an "ah."

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:45.560 align:middle line:84%
But thanks to Delaware,
we know that it's an "au."

00:45:45.560 --> 00:45:49.590 align:middle line:84%
So Delaware has a related word
and it has a long "ah" there,

00:45:49.590 --> 00:45:52.982 align:middle line:84%
and that tells us that this
is Wampanoag "wusomee."

00:45:52.982 --> 00:45:54.440 align:middle line:84%
And being able to
figure things out

00:45:54.440 --> 00:45:57.530 align:middle line:84%
like that then helps us figure
more things out about how

00:45:57.530 --> 00:45:59.330 align:middle line:90%
their spelling system works.

00:45:59.330 --> 00:46:02.630 align:middle line:84%
So there's this ongoing
process of putting together

00:46:02.630 --> 00:46:06.800 align:middle line:84%
what the heck they're
trying to spell.

00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:09.988 align:middle line:84%
We also think we know where
stress goes in this word.

00:46:09.988 --> 00:46:11.780 align:middle line:84%
That is, we think that
the word is stressed

00:46:11.780 --> 00:46:12.738 align:middle line:90%
on its second syllable.

00:46:12.738 --> 00:46:13.670 align:middle line:90%
It's "wu-SO-mee."

00:46:13.670 --> 00:46:15.350 align:middle line:90%
So it's not "WU-so-mee."

00:46:15.350 --> 00:46:17.405 align:middle line:90%
And it's not "wu-so-MEE."

00:46:17.405 --> 00:46:19.280 align:middle line:84%
And I want to tell you
a little bit about how

00:46:19.280 --> 00:46:22.190 align:middle line:84%
we think we know that because
we've got a little bit of time

00:46:22.190 --> 00:46:24.470 align:middle line:90%
left here.

00:46:24.470 --> 00:46:26.180 align:middle line:84%
This story goes back
a little further.

00:46:26.180 --> 00:46:30.382 align:middle line:84%
In 1640, the first
book was published

00:46:30.382 --> 00:46:32.090 align:middle line:84%
in the British colonies
in North America.

00:46:32.090 --> 00:46:33.800 align:middle line:84%
It was published also
here in Cambridge.

00:46:33.800 --> 00:46:35.960 align:middle line:90%
It's called the Bay Psalm Book.

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:38.510 align:middle line:84%
And it is a translation
of the Book of Psalms

00:46:38.510 --> 00:46:41.780 align:middle line:90%
into English metrical verse.

00:46:41.780 --> 00:46:45.260 align:middle line:84%
So here, for example,
is the Psalm 1:1,

00:46:45.260 --> 00:46:46.790 align:middle line:90%
the first verse of Psalms.

00:46:46.790 --> 00:46:48.260 align:middle line:84%
So in the King
James version, which

00:46:48.260 --> 00:46:50.660 align:middle line:84%
is what those people would
have been working with,

00:46:50.660 --> 00:46:52.160 align:middle line:84%
you've got, over
there on the left,

00:46:52.160 --> 00:46:53.750 align:middle line:84%
"Blessed is the man
that walketh not

00:46:53.750 --> 00:46:56.270 align:middle line:84%
in the council of the ungodly,
nor standeth in the way

00:46:56.270 --> 00:46:59.570 align:middle line:84%
of sinners, nor sitteth in
the seat of the scornful."

00:46:59.570 --> 00:47:02.840 align:middle line:84%
The Bay Psalm book goes, "O
Blessed man that in th'advice /

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:05.960 align:middle line:84%
of wicked doth not walk, /
nor stand in sinner's way,

00:47:05.960 --> 00:47:08.110 align:middle line:84%
nor sit / in chair
of scornful folk."

00:47:08.110 --> 00:47:10.550 align:middle line:90%
And it goes on and on like that.

00:47:10.550 --> 00:47:13.010 align:middle line:84%
The point of the
Bay Psalm Book--

00:47:13.010 --> 00:47:17.120 align:middle line:84%
or here's the beginning of the
23rd Psalm, which some of you

00:47:17.120 --> 00:47:17.660 align:middle line:90%
may know.

00:47:17.660 --> 00:47:18.932 align:middle line:90%
So "The Lord is my shepherd.

00:47:18.932 --> 00:47:19.640 align:middle line:90%
I shall not want.

00:47:19.640 --> 00:47:21.770 align:middle line:84%
He maketh me to lie down
in green pastures...."

00:47:21.770 --> 00:47:23.510 align:middle line:84%
In the Bay Psalm
Book, that one goes,

00:47:23.510 --> 00:47:26.090 align:middle line:84%
"The Lord to me a shepherd
is, / want therefore

00:47:26.090 --> 00:47:28.755 align:middle line:84%
shall not I. / He in the
fields of tender grass

00:47:28.755 --> 00:47:31.290 align:middle line:84%
/ doth cause me down
to lie" and so on.

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:34.700 align:middle line:84%
So this is another translation
of the Book of Psalms

00:47:34.700 --> 00:47:37.670 align:middle line:90%
into rhymed metrical verse.

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:39.770 align:middle line:84%
The idea was to create
things that people

00:47:39.770 --> 00:47:41.585 align:middle line:90%
could sing in church.

00:47:41.585 --> 00:47:44.210 align:middle line:84%
So this was something that John
Calvin had apparently proposed,

00:47:44.210 --> 00:47:45.410 align:middle line:90%
the founder of Calvinism.

00:47:45.410 --> 00:47:46.970 align:middle line:84%
He suggested that
the Book of Psalms

00:47:46.970 --> 00:47:49.970 align:middle line:84%
should be translated into
English in metrical verse.

00:47:49.970 --> 00:47:52.310 align:middle line:84%
I guess these were
people who were

00:47:52.310 --> 00:47:55.040 align:middle line:84%
a little worried that
music might be sinful

00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:57.110 align:middle line:84%
or might lead to
sin, and so the idea

00:47:57.110 --> 00:47:59.540 align:middle line:84%
was that if you allowed
God to provide the lyrics,

00:47:59.540 --> 00:48:01.640 align:middle line:84%
that presumably there
were limits on how sinful

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:02.300 align:middle line:90%
you could be.

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:04.820 align:middle line:90%
So they had these translations.

00:48:04.820 --> 00:48:06.590 align:middle line:84%
The Bay Psalm Book
was hugely popular.

00:48:06.590 --> 00:48:10.130 align:middle line:90%
It went through many printings.

00:48:10.130 --> 00:48:14.540 align:middle line:84%
It starts with this
nice cute introduction

00:48:14.540 --> 00:48:15.980 align:middle line:84%
in which they say
something like--

00:48:15.980 --> 00:48:18.470 align:middle line:84%
because it wasn't the
only translation of Psalms

00:48:18.470 --> 00:48:21.848 align:middle line:90%
into metrical verse.

00:48:21.848 --> 00:48:24.140 align:middle line:84%
They say something in their
introduction like, you may,

00:48:24.140 --> 00:48:25.040 align:middle line:90%
if you compare us--

00:48:25.040 --> 00:48:26.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm paraphrasing-- they
say, if you compare us

00:48:26.990 --> 00:48:28.940 align:middle line:84%
with other translations
into metrical verse,

00:48:28.940 --> 00:48:31.610 align:middle line:84%
you may notice that our poetry
is not as nice as theirs,

00:48:31.610 --> 00:48:33.890 align:middle line:84%
and that's because we
were more concerned

00:48:33.890 --> 00:48:35.900 align:middle line:90%
with literal accuracy.

00:48:35.900 --> 00:48:38.620 align:middle line:84%
So it's not because we're lousy
poets or anything like that.

00:48:38.620 --> 00:48:42.350 align:middle line:84%
It's because we were shooting
for being exactly the--

00:48:42.350 --> 00:48:44.960 align:middle line:90%
accurate to the original.

00:48:44.960 --> 00:48:48.650 align:middle line:84%
John Eliot, who later was one
of the translators of the Bible

00:48:48.650 --> 00:48:51.855 align:middle line:84%
into Wampanoag, was one of the
writers of the Bay Psalm Book.

00:48:51.855 --> 00:48:54.230 align:middle line:84%
So he was involved in the
writing of the Bay Psalm Book--

00:48:54.230 --> 00:48:57.710 align:middle line:90%
one of several people who was.

00:48:57.710 --> 00:49:02.540 align:middle line:84%
Here's the Wampanoag translation
for the first verse of Psalms.

00:49:02.540 --> 00:49:05.070 align:middle line:84%
You've got it down there
in the middle of this page.

00:49:05.070 --> 00:49:07.070 align:middle line:84%
And as you can see,
down underneath it,

00:49:07.070 --> 00:49:10.580 align:middle line:84%
I have a literal translation
of the Wampanoag words.

00:49:10.580 --> 00:49:12.050 align:middle line:84%
And the only point
to make here is

00:49:12.050 --> 00:49:13.925 align:middle line:84%
that the literal
translation of the Wampanoag

00:49:13.925 --> 00:49:16.250 align:middle line:84%
words-- it's more or less
a word-for-word translation

00:49:16.250 --> 00:49:19.040 align:middle line:84%
of the King James Version in
more or less the same order.

00:49:19.040 --> 00:49:22.250 align:middle line:90%


00:49:22.250 --> 00:49:24.410 align:middle line:84%
So on the one hand,
the fact that they

00:49:24.410 --> 00:49:27.680 align:middle line:84%
were sticking to the English
word order most of the time

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:29.600 align:middle line:84%
is generally true in
the Bible translation,

00:49:29.600 --> 00:49:31.070 align:middle line:90%
not just in Psalms.

00:49:31.070 --> 00:49:32.570 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, it's
kind of frustrating.

00:49:32.570 --> 00:49:35.463 align:middle line:84%
You wish that you-- because
we know from other Algonquian

00:49:35.463 --> 00:49:37.130 align:middle line:84%
languages that Wampanoag
word order must

00:49:37.130 --> 00:49:38.612 align:middle line:90%
have been pretty free.

00:49:38.612 --> 00:49:40.070 align:middle line:84%
One nice thing
about it, though, is

00:49:40.070 --> 00:49:43.545 align:middle line:84%
that when they diverge from
the English word order,

00:49:43.545 --> 00:49:45.170 align:middle line:84%
like when they put
something in a place

00:49:45.170 --> 00:49:47.660 align:middle line:84%
where it isn't in
the English, that's

00:49:47.660 --> 00:49:51.410 align:middle line:84%
more or less a very clear sign
that the document is giving us

00:49:51.410 --> 00:49:53.000 align:middle line:84%
that the English
word order would

00:49:53.000 --> 00:49:54.620 align:middle line:84%
have been ungrammatical
in Wampanoag,

00:49:54.620 --> 00:49:55.860 align:middle line:90%
which is kind of nice.

00:49:55.860 --> 00:49:59.340 align:middle line:84%
So usually, you can't learn that
kind of thing from a document.

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:01.533 align:middle line:84%
A document just has a
bunch of sentences in it

00:50:01.533 --> 00:50:03.200 align:middle line:84%
and you can go through
it and figure out

00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:06.620 align:middle line:84%
which word orders are common
and which ones are uncommon.

00:50:06.620 --> 00:50:08.870 align:middle line:84%
In this kind of case,
whenever Wampanoag

00:50:08.870 --> 00:50:10.550 align:middle line:84%
doesn't do the
English word order,

00:50:10.550 --> 00:50:12.050 align:middle line:84%
the document is
actually telling us,

00:50:12.050 --> 00:50:13.842 align:middle line:84%
the English word order
would have been bad,

00:50:13.842 --> 00:50:15.180 align:middle line:90%
which is kind of nice.

00:50:15.180 --> 00:50:18.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
Wampanoag translation.

00:50:18.170 --> 00:50:20.960 align:middle line:84%
But at the end of
the Bible, there's

00:50:20.960 --> 00:50:23.428 align:middle line:90%
another Wampanoag translation.

00:50:23.428 --> 00:50:24.470 align:middle line:90%
I'll just read it to you.

00:50:24.470 --> 00:50:32.420 align:middle line:84%
It goes [SPEAKING WAMPANOAG]----
and so on and so on.

00:50:32.420 --> 00:50:34.580 align:middle line:84%
It's another
metrical translation

00:50:34.580 --> 00:50:37.730 align:middle line:90%
of the Book of Psalms.

00:50:37.730 --> 00:50:39.950 align:middle line:84%
Using the same rules,
it's alternating

00:50:39.950 --> 00:50:41.210 align:middle line:90%
long and short lines--

00:50:41.210 --> 00:50:42.860 align:middle line:84%
eight-syllable and
six-syllable lines

00:50:42.860 --> 00:50:45.920 align:middle line:84%
with the six-syllable lines
rhyming with each other

00:50:45.920 --> 00:50:48.440 align:middle line:84%
and the eight-syllable
lines not.

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:53.660 align:middle line:84%
And it deviates from the English
word order very seriously--

00:50:53.660 --> 00:50:54.860 align:middle line:90%
changes all kinds of things.

00:50:54.860 --> 00:50:58.120 align:middle line:90%


00:50:58.120 --> 00:51:01.592 align:middle line:84%
So when I first discovered this,
I was looking through the Bible

00:51:01.592 --> 00:51:02.800 align:middle line:90%
and I found this translation.

00:51:02.800 --> 00:51:04.030 align:middle line:84%
I was like, oh, this
looks like poetry.

00:51:04.030 --> 00:51:04.870 align:middle line:90%
What is this?

00:51:04.870 --> 00:51:06.892 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, oh,
it rhymes, hot dog.

00:51:06.892 --> 00:51:09.100 align:middle line:84%
And I thought, oh, cool,
I'm going to learn something

00:51:09.100 --> 00:51:10.255 align:middle line:90%
about how stress worked.

00:51:10.255 --> 00:51:12.850 align:middle line:90%


00:51:12.850 --> 00:51:17.110 align:middle line:84%
So how do you know whether
to say the word for too much.

00:51:17.110 --> 00:51:19.600 align:middle line:84%
I think now it's "wu-SOM-ee,"
with stress on the second

00:51:19.600 --> 00:51:20.110 align:middle line:90%
syllable.

00:51:20.110 --> 00:51:21.080 align:middle line:90%
How do I know that?

00:51:21.080 --> 00:51:23.205 align:middle line:84%
How do I know it's not
stress on the first syllable

00:51:23.205 --> 00:51:24.850 align:middle line:90%
or the third?

00:51:24.850 --> 00:51:29.290 align:middle line:84%
Eliot also wrote in English
a grammar of Wampanoag,

00:51:29.290 --> 00:51:33.400 align:middle line:84%
in which he laid out the
principles of grammar--

00:51:33.400 --> 00:51:34.450 align:middle line:90%
very sensibly, actually.

00:51:34.450 --> 00:51:35.710 align:middle line:90%
It's a really nice document.

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:38.778 align:middle line:90%
He talks about a lot of things.

00:51:38.778 --> 00:51:40.570 align:middle line:84%
And one of the things
he says very early on

00:51:40.570 --> 00:51:42.460 align:middle line:84%
is I'm going to indicate
stress because it's

00:51:42.460 --> 00:51:44.890 align:middle line:84%
very important to put the
stress in the right places

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:47.380 align:middle line:84%
in the words if you
want to be understood.

00:51:47.380 --> 00:51:49.840 align:middle line:84%
And he gives one example,
the word for dog.

00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:52.210 align:middle line:84%
He says the word
for dog is stressed

00:51:52.210 --> 00:51:53.170 align:middle line:90%
on its second syllable.

00:51:53.170 --> 00:51:54.700 align:middle line:90%
It's "anum."

00:51:54.700 --> 00:51:57.010 align:middle line:84%
And then he never
mentioned stress again

00:51:57.010 --> 00:51:59.930 align:middle line:90%
or indicates it anywhere else.

00:51:59.930 --> 00:52:03.480 align:middle line:84%
And I could just kill
him, except he's dead.

00:52:03.480 --> 00:52:05.230 align:middle line:84%
And so when I found
this poem, I was like,

00:52:05.230 --> 00:52:08.182 align:middle line:84%
"Ha, here's a place where
I'm going to find out where

00:52:08.182 --> 00:52:09.850 align:middle line:90%
the Wampanoag put stress--

00:52:09.850 --> 00:52:11.890 align:middle line:84%
what are the rules
for Wampanoag stress??

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:15.580 align:middle line:84%
And so I started going through,
starting with the assumption

00:52:15.580 --> 00:52:19.940 align:middle line:84%
that he was always getting
the stress in the right place,

00:52:19.940 --> 00:52:21.988 align:middle line:90%
which is false, it turns out.

00:52:21.988 --> 00:52:23.530 align:middle line:84%
So if you start with
that assumption,

00:52:23.530 --> 00:52:25.390 align:middle line:84%
you pretty quickly
come to the conclusion

00:52:25.390 --> 00:52:28.330 align:middle line:84%
there are lots and
lots of contradictions.

00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:29.740 align:middle line:90%
He's not doing that.

00:52:29.740 --> 00:52:32.170 align:middle line:84%
And to be fair to
him here, he is

00:52:32.170 --> 00:52:34.000 align:middle line:84%
trying to translate
the Book of Psalms

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:36.550 align:middle line:84%
into a language that's
not his native language.

00:52:36.550 --> 00:52:37.810 align:middle line:90%
He wants it to rhyme.

00:52:37.810 --> 00:52:39.085 align:middle line:90%
He wants the words to be--

00:52:39.085 --> 00:52:41.650 align:middle line:84%
the lines to be
the right length.

00:52:41.650 --> 00:52:44.590 align:middle line:84%
He wants it to accurately
represent, more or less,

00:52:44.590 --> 00:52:47.050 align:middle line:90%
what's in Psalms.

00:52:47.050 --> 00:52:50.210 align:middle line:84%
Meter is not-- it's somewhere
on this list of priorities,

00:52:50.210 --> 00:52:54.250 align:middle line:84%
but it's not the highest thing
on his list of priorities.

00:52:54.250 --> 00:52:58.450 align:middle line:90%


00:52:58.450 --> 00:53:00.340 align:middle line:84%
So what I eventually
figured out was

00:53:00.340 --> 00:53:02.770 align:middle line:84%
there is something we can
learn from the metrical Psalms,

00:53:02.770 --> 00:53:04.300 align:middle line:84%
and I can illustrate
this for you

00:53:04.300 --> 00:53:06.080 align:middle line:84%
by going back to
the English ones.

00:53:06.080 --> 00:53:11.590 align:middle line:84%
So here's the beginning of the
23rd Psalm in the King James

00:53:11.590 --> 00:53:13.660 align:middle line:84%
version and in the
Bay Psalm Book.

00:53:13.660 --> 00:53:16.820 align:middle line:84%
In the Bay Psalm Book,
they've made various changes.

00:53:16.820 --> 00:53:20.330 align:middle line:84%
So let's think about
some of these changes.

00:53:20.330 --> 00:53:24.544 align:middle line:84%
Why did they change
"I shall not want"

00:53:24.544 --> 00:53:27.130 align:middle line:90%
to "want therefore shall not I?"

00:53:27.130 --> 00:53:28.630 align:middle line:90%
Why did they do that?

00:53:28.630 --> 00:53:30.430 align:middle line:84%
It's not the normal
English word order.

00:53:30.430 --> 00:53:34.840 align:middle line:90%


00:53:34.840 --> 00:53:37.020 align:middle line:84%
So that they ended up
with "The Lord to me

00:53:37.020 --> 00:53:38.790 align:middle line:84%
a shepherd is, /
want therefore shall

00:53:38.790 --> 00:53:41.460 align:middle line:84%
not I. / He in the
folds of tender grass /

00:53:41.460 --> 00:53:43.930 align:middle line:90%
doth cause me down to lie."

00:53:43.930 --> 00:53:48.070 align:middle line:84%
Why did they want to change
the order in that second line?

00:53:48.070 --> 00:53:48.888 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

00:53:48.888 --> 00:53:50.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It has to do
with the positioning

00:53:50.680 --> 00:53:54.218 align:middle line:84%
and the stress in the words so
that fits a poetic [INAUDIBLE]..

00:53:54.218 --> 00:53:56.260 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well, so
they do that sometimes.

00:53:56.260 --> 00:53:58.343 align:middle line:84%
In this particular case,
there's something simpler

00:53:58.343 --> 00:53:59.210 align:middle line:90%
that they're doing.

00:53:59.210 --> 00:53:59.765 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:53:59.765 --> 00:54:01.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean, it's
"I" rhymes with "lie."

00:54:01.640 --> 00:54:03.470 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
they're setting up a rhyme.

00:54:03.470 --> 00:54:04.928 align:middle line:84%
So they looked at
the line and they

00:54:04.928 --> 00:54:07.880 align:middle line:84%
wanted two things that
would end in the same sound,

00:54:07.880 --> 00:54:09.770 align:middle line:84%
and they decided
on "I" and "lie."

00:54:09.770 --> 00:54:13.247 align:middle line:84%
That's it exactly,
for this pair.

00:54:13.247 --> 00:54:15.830 align:middle line:84%
There's another place, though,
where they do the kind of thing

00:54:15.830 --> 00:54:17.490 align:middle line:90%
that Joseph is talking about.

00:54:17.490 --> 00:54:20.180 align:middle line:84%
So "He leadeth with me
beside the still waters."

00:54:20.180 --> 00:54:24.102 align:middle line:84%
They change that to "To
waters calm me gently leads,"

00:54:24.102 --> 00:54:26.060 align:middle line:84%
which has all kinds of
entertaining properties.

00:54:26.060 --> 00:54:28.280 align:middle line:84%
But one of them is that
the adjective "calm"

00:54:28.280 --> 00:54:30.800 align:middle line:90%
comes after the noun "waters."

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:33.960 align:middle line:84%
Why did they say "to waters
calm me gently leads?"

00:54:33.960 --> 00:54:38.740 align:middle line:84%
Why didn't they say "to calm
waters me gently leads?"

00:54:38.740 --> 00:54:41.160 align:middle line:84%
And now we get to
recycle Joseph's answer.

00:54:41.160 --> 00:54:45.240 align:middle line:84%
So "to waters calm--"
you get to put stress

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:47.340 align:middle line:84%
on the second and fourth
syllables of that.

00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:50.820 align:middle line:84%
Stress in English "waters"
goes on the first syllable.

00:54:50.820 --> 00:54:52.380 align:middle line:84%
And then you get to
stress on "calm."

00:54:52.380 --> 00:54:54.253 align:middle line:84%
If it were "to
calm waters," well,

00:54:54.253 --> 00:54:56.670 align:middle line:84%
then you'd have stress on the
second and third syllables--

00:54:56.670 --> 00:54:57.660 align:middle line:90%
"to calm waters."

00:54:57.660 --> 00:54:58.990 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:54:58.990 --> 00:55:01.920 align:middle line:84%
So if you're trying to get
a line in which you have

00:55:01.920 --> 00:55:04.150 align:middle line:84%
alternating stresses--
which is what he's doing,

00:55:04.150 --> 00:55:07.740 align:middle line:84%
he wants the stresses to be
on the even-numbered beats--

00:55:07.740 --> 00:55:10.050 align:middle line:84%
then changing the order of
the adjective and the noun

00:55:10.050 --> 00:55:10.560 align:middle line:90%
makes sense.

00:55:10.560 --> 00:55:12.210 align:middle line:90%
That's what you're shooting for.

00:55:12.210 --> 00:55:13.250 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:55:13.250 --> 00:55:14.333 align:middle line:90%
That's what they're doing.

00:55:14.333 --> 00:55:16.430 align:middle line:90%


00:55:16.430 --> 00:55:18.880 align:middle line:84%
So I hope it makes
sense because what

00:55:18.880 --> 00:55:21.700 align:middle line:84%
I ended up doing was looking for
things like that in Wampanoag,

00:55:21.700 --> 00:55:24.070 align:middle line:84%
and I'll show you an
example of what I found,

00:55:24.070 --> 00:55:26.240 align:middle line:90%
and then I'll let you go.

00:55:26.240 --> 00:55:30.070 align:middle line:84%
Here's an example from
the King James version,

00:55:30.070 --> 00:55:33.100 align:middle line:84%
from 22:16, "dogs
have compassed me.

00:55:33.100 --> 00:55:34.990 align:middle line:84%
The assembly of the
wicked have enclosed me.

00:55:34.990 --> 00:55:38.020 align:middle line:84%
They pierced my
hands and my feet."

00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:40.540 align:middle line:84%
And I'm bold-facing
that because I want

00:55:40.540 --> 00:55:42.100 align:middle line:90%
you to pay attention to it.

00:55:42.100 --> 00:55:45.490 align:middle line:84%
Here's the nonpoetic
Wampanoag translation

00:55:45.490 --> 00:55:49.030 align:middle line:84%
of that, which is more or less
a word-for-word translation

00:55:49.030 --> 00:55:50.020 align:middle line:90%
of the English.

00:55:50.020 --> 00:55:52.270 align:middle line:84%
There's "dog" again,
and I'm in the plural,

00:55:52.270 --> 00:55:55.660 align:middle line:90%
"anumwak," in the first line.

00:55:55.660 --> 00:55:58.495 align:middle line:84%
So "dogs have compassed me--"
that means "encircled me--"

00:55:58.495 --> 00:56:00.370 align:middle line:84%
"the assembly of the
wicked have enclosed me.

00:56:00.370 --> 00:56:01.990 align:middle line:84%
They pierced my
hands and my feet."

00:56:01.990 --> 00:56:05.080 align:middle line:84%
And that Wampanoag line ends
with the Wampanoag words

00:56:05.080 --> 00:56:06.760 align:middle line:90%
for my hands and my feet.

00:56:06.760 --> 00:56:09.160 align:middle line:90%
And that's [SPEAKING WAMPANOAG].

00:56:09.160 --> 00:56:11.290 align:middle line:84%
But in the poetic
translation they

00:56:11.290 --> 00:56:12.970 align:middle line:84%
changed it to "my
feet and my hands"?

00:56:12.970 --> 00:56:15.570 align:middle line:90%


00:56:15.570 --> 00:56:16.930 align:middle line:90%
Why did they do that?

00:56:16.930 --> 00:56:19.320 align:middle line:84%
Well, they didn't
do it to set up

00:56:19.320 --> 00:56:22.320 align:middle line:84%
a rhyme because the rhyming
lines are the second

00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:23.255 align:middle line:90%
and the fourth lines.

00:56:23.255 --> 00:56:24.630 align:middle line:84%
And the first and
the third lines

00:56:24.630 --> 00:56:25.810 align:middle line:90%
don't rhyme with each other.

00:56:25.810 --> 00:56:27.810 align:middle line:84%
It's the short lines that
rhyme with each other.

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:34.360 align:middle line:84%
So hypothesis-- they
wanted them in that order

00:56:34.360 --> 00:56:36.480 align:middle line:84%
in order to get the
stresses in the right place.

00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:39.660 align:middle line:84%
So if you imagine that the
stresses go on the even

00:56:39.660 --> 00:56:43.490 align:middle line:84%
syllables, there was something
that he liked better about

00:56:43.490 --> 00:56:47.370 align:middle line:84%
[SPEAKING WAMPANOAG],, which
is "my feet and my hands"--

00:56:47.370 --> 00:56:52.510 align:middle line:84%
that he preferred that
to [SPEAKING WAMPANOAG]..

00:56:52.510 --> 00:56:54.950 align:middle line:84%
So if you put stresses
on the even syllables,

00:56:54.950 --> 00:56:56.350 align:middle line:90%
that's where they would go.

00:56:56.350 --> 00:56:59.260 align:middle line:84%
The word for "my hands,"
"an-netchi-tash,"

00:56:59.260 --> 00:57:01.970 align:middle line:84%
that gets the same stress in
either of those possibilities.

00:57:01.970 --> 00:57:04.840 align:middle line:84%
So this is just comparing
"my feet and my hands"

00:57:04.840 --> 00:57:06.070 align:middle line:90%
with "my hands and my feet."

00:57:06.070 --> 00:57:07.870 align:middle line:84%
And we're asking,
where would stresses

00:57:07.870 --> 00:57:10.180 align:middle line:84%
go in those two
versions of the line?

00:57:10.180 --> 00:57:14.020 align:middle line:84%
In the word for "my hands,"
which is "nuh-nuh-chi-kash,"

00:57:14.020 --> 00:57:16.390 align:middle line:84%
the stress is going in the
same places in both of those

00:57:16.390 --> 00:57:19.270 align:middle line:84%
versions of the line, so we
can't learn anything about that

00:57:19.270 --> 00:57:22.640 align:middle line:84%
word, where stress goes in
that word, by this choice.

00:57:22.640 --> 00:57:24.598 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter which
of those things he did.

00:57:24.598 --> 00:57:26.182 align:middle line:84%
He would have stress
in the same place

00:57:26.182 --> 00:57:27.430 align:middle line:90%
in the word for "my hands."

00:57:27.430 --> 00:57:30.430 align:middle line:84%
But in the word for "my feet,"
what he's doing is choosing

00:57:30.430 --> 00:57:33.340 align:middle line:84%
between "nuh-see-tash,"
stress on the second syllable,

00:57:33.340 --> 00:57:35.980 align:middle line:84%
and NUH-see-TASH," stress on
the first and third syllables.

00:57:35.980 --> 00:57:38.680 align:middle line:84%
And he's choosing stress
on the second syllable.

00:57:38.680 --> 00:57:41.830 align:middle line:84%
So hypothesis-- this is teaching
us that stress actually goes

00:57:41.830 --> 00:57:45.350 align:middle line:84%
on the second syllable of
my feet, "nuh-see-tash."

00:57:45.350 --> 00:57:49.330 align:middle line:84%
So if you are crazy
enough to read

00:57:49.330 --> 00:57:53.260 align:middle line:84%
the poetic version of the
Wampanoag songs line by line,

00:57:53.260 --> 00:57:57.040 align:middle line:84%
looking for verses where the
word order has been changed

00:57:57.040 --> 00:58:01.960 align:middle line:84%
in a way that doesn't help
with setting up a rhyme,

00:58:01.960 --> 00:58:03.820 align:middle line:84%
looking for things
like this, then

00:58:03.820 --> 00:58:07.060 align:middle line:84%
you end up with a
list of words where

00:58:07.060 --> 00:58:09.610 align:middle line:84%
you have a guess about
where stress goes.

00:58:09.610 --> 00:58:12.020 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at all
of those words, you--

00:58:12.020 --> 00:58:14.330 align:middle line:84%
and I am in fact crazy
enough to do that.

00:58:14.330 --> 00:58:20.170 align:middle line:84%
I did it-- you end up with
a set of words for which

00:58:20.170 --> 00:58:22.177 align:middle line:84%
where stress goes,
and you can develop

00:58:22.177 --> 00:58:23.260 align:middle line:90%
a theory of where it goes.

00:58:23.260 --> 00:58:25.052 align:middle line:84%
It turns out the rules
for Wampanoag stress

00:58:25.052 --> 00:58:27.220 align:middle line:84%
are quite similar to
the rules for stress

00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:29.500 align:middle line:84%
in Delaware, which is the
language I was showing you

00:58:29.500 --> 00:58:30.243 align:middle line:90%
earlier.

00:58:30.243 --> 00:58:32.410 align:middle line:84%
It's the kind of thing we
could have hoped was true,

00:58:32.410 --> 00:58:36.280 align:middle line:84%
but it's nice to see
that it actually is true.

00:58:36.280 --> 00:58:39.070 align:middle line:84%
I'll show you one more slide,
and then I'll let you go.

00:58:39.070 --> 00:58:41.470 align:middle line:84%
Wampanoag is a
polysynthetic language.

00:58:41.470 --> 00:58:45.580 align:middle line:84%
Here's a letter from
Experience Mayhew,

00:58:45.580 --> 00:58:50.320 align:middle line:84%
who was another missionary who
grew up on Martha's Vineyard.

00:58:50.320 --> 00:58:51.820 align:middle line:84%
He was the child
of missionaries who

00:58:51.820 --> 00:58:54.237 align:middle line:84%
grew up on Martha's Vineyard,
by his own account bilingual

00:58:54.237 --> 00:58:55.780 align:middle line:90%
in English and Wampanoag.

00:58:55.780 --> 00:58:59.020 align:middle line:84%
We have one letter
from him to a friend

00:58:59.020 --> 00:59:01.990 align:middle line:84%
who had asked him various
questions about the language.

00:59:01.990 --> 00:59:04.180 align:middle line:84%
He begins his reply
by apologizing

00:59:04.180 --> 00:59:07.420 align:middle line:84%
for being slow to respond
to the other guy's letter

00:59:07.420 --> 00:59:09.910 align:middle line:84%
because several members of his
family, including, I think,

00:59:09.910 --> 00:59:11.452 align:middle line:84%
his wife and several
of his children,

00:59:11.452 --> 00:59:12.850 align:middle line:90%
had died from the plague.

00:59:12.850 --> 00:59:15.370 align:middle line:84%
And he's like, "I'm
sorry, I've been busy.

00:59:15.370 --> 00:59:18.610 align:middle line:84%
But here are the answers to
your linguistic questions."

00:59:18.610 --> 00:59:21.820 align:middle line:84%
And one of the points he
makes is this language

00:59:21.820 --> 00:59:23.448 align:middle line:90%
goes in for a really long words.

00:59:23.448 --> 00:59:25.990 align:middle line:84%
The guy had asked him, I guess,
for the longest word he could

00:59:25.990 --> 00:59:28.115 align:middle line:84%
think of, and the one he
came up with is down there

00:59:28.115 --> 00:59:30.850 align:middle line:84%
at the bottom, "our well-skilled
looking glass makers--"

00:59:30.850 --> 00:59:32.920 align:middle line:84%
that is, our skillful
mirror makers--

00:59:32.920 --> 00:59:34.630 align:middle line:90%
[SPEAKING WAMPANOAG]

00:59:34.630 --> 00:59:37.330 align:middle line:90%


00:59:37.330 --> 00:59:40.060 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, it's an extremely
polysynthetic language.

00:59:40.060 --> 00:59:43.240 align:middle line:84%
There's the break
down for that word.

00:59:43.240 --> 00:59:46.060 align:middle line:84%
Questions about Wampanoag
or endangered languages

00:59:46.060 --> 00:59:48.900 align:middle line:90%
or any of this stuff?

00:59:48.900 --> 00:59:51.460 align:middle line:84%
OK, this is probably a
good place for us to stop.

00:59:51.460 --> 00:59:54.080 align:middle line:90%
So let's stop here, and I--

